[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479078 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dcc6f5fd-050d-5fbb-88f7-e841559dfb66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478906}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dcc6f5fd-050d-5fbb-88f7-e841559dfb66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478906} :answer-field/id #uuid "af98e227-3993-52d7-9ae5-2d273b174754" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479027}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "32e41319-b064-5c4a-91f7-13675a0d7fcb" :question/math-academy-id "q-174388" :question/problem "Expressed as a fraction in lowest terms, $\\frac{4}{3}÷2-\\frac{1}{3}=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nFirst, we divide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3}÷2-\\frac{1}{3} &= (\\frac{4}{3}÷\\frac{2}{1})-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ &= (\\frac{4}{3}\\times \\frac{1}{2})-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ &= (\\frac{4\\times 1}{3\\times 2})-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ &= \\frac{4}{6}-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we subtract:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{6}-\\frac{1}{3} &= \\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ &= \\frac{2-1}{3} \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479087 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "faed038c-cb2b-58d2-9845-181cd5acb89c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478951}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "faed038c-cb2b-58d2-9845-181cd5acb89c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478951} :answer-field/id #uuid "04be7bca-6977-5bf8-9d2e-5320d2ebe561" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478960}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4e17c219-57c5-563f-87a2-8e9738ef4bd2" :question/math-academy-id "q-178619" :question/problem "Expressed as a fraction in its simplest form, $\\frac{5}{8}÷(-\\frac{3}{4})=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Dividing a positive number by a negative number gives a negative number. Hence, the result will be negative.\n\nTherefore, since\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{8}÷\\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{5\\times 4}{8\\times 3}=\\frac{20}{24}=\\frac{5}{6},\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{6}÷(-\\frac{3}{4})=-\\frac{5}{6}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479096 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d396ebfa-2351-59b3-9a9a-85e9a027b858" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478895}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d396ebfa-2351-59b3-9a9a-85e9a027b858" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478895} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6b9cf49-c2bb-5ce4-941d-b3540c075320" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479024}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "720361bc-07a5-589b-85d2-04870e7c2310" :question/math-academy-id "q-176386" :question/problem "Expressed as an integer, $8÷(-\\frac{4}{5})-3=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nFirst, we divide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8÷(-\\frac{4}{5})-3 &= (8÷(-\\frac{4}{5}))-3 \\\\ &= (\\frac{8}{1}\\times (-\\frac{5}{4}))-3 \\\\ &= -\\frac{8\\times 5}{1\\times 4}-3 \\\\ &= -\\frac{40}{4}-3 \\\\ &= -10-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we subtract:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-10-3 & =-13\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479098 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46fc3904-dcb6-59e4-81f2-1c98ffc824a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{5}^{8}\\times \\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478659} {:answer/id #uuid "5d5a8e43-fd30-5edd-ba7b-b1bb83e575da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{5}^{8}\\times {(\\frac{2}{3})}^{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478690} {:answer/id #uuid "891f613b-d616-5d3e-a719-e836482dadb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\times \\frac{{2}^{8}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478772} {:answer/id #uuid "df541607-6c70-590d-bee4-e40a6d04081e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{2}^{8}\\times {2}^{8}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478909} {:answer/id #uuid "e4abdcf6-267a-5e24-812d-75bc167c567a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\times {(\\frac{2}{3})}^{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478914}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d5a8e43-fd30-5edd-ba7b-b1bb83e575da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{5}^{8}\\times {(\\frac{2}{3})}^{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478690} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd4e9dc7-33c7-511c-9f0b-4cbc6bec4a32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479032}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "79195c97-f47a-5a8c-930f-60cd5aaabae5" :question/math-academy-id "q-68613" :question/problem "${(5\\times \\frac{2}{3})}^{8}=$" :question/worked-solution "To raise the product of two numbers to the power of $8$, we can raise each number to the power of $8,$ and then multiply:\n\n$$\n{(5\\times \\frac{2}{3})}^{8}={5}^{8}\\times {(\\frac{2}{3})}^{8}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479099 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f1d2384-537e-5cdc-89d2-9514f7f915b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478578} {:answer/id #uuid "4213e743-fdee-55e2-9a6f-9b709160ce8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478650} {:answer/id #uuid "68a6cf61-7ae6-5cd9-b610-75dedb0da673" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478714} {:answer/id #uuid "74631ce5-9bb8-5134-9259-1cb8106e5159" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478730} {:answer/id #uuid "b27fd78c-cdb2-5780-a4d0-dca6453eaa9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478848}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4213e743-fdee-55e2-9a6f-9b709160ce8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478650} :answer-field/id #uuid "86ccf1cc-db7d-5bc8-961d-8e2cd5b455cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479012}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "791eebb0-2b19-5413-9776-2b792618848a" :question/math-academy-id "q-104802" :question/problem "![](images/01/0115d90518526da279492096da66497c3ea1e98cbff14e66657bb08c11ea479b.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{′}(-2)$ where $h(x)=f(x)g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "The product and addition rules for derivatives give\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)={f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)+f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(-2)={f}^{′}(-2)\\cdot g(-2)+f(-2)\\cdot {g}^{′}(-2).\n$$\n\nFrom the graphs above, we obtain that $f(-2)=2$ and $g(-2)=3.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (-4,0)$ equals $-1.$ So, ${f}^{′}(-2)=-1.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (-4,0)$ equals $-\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, ${g}^{′}(-2)=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(-2) & ={f}^{′}(-2)\\cdot g(-2)+f(-2)\\cdot {g}^{′}(-2) \\\\  & =-1\\cdot 3+2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479102 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48f99d44-4d8f-5e63-a4f9-ad7e2a143699" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478663} {:answer/id #uuid "4f3cc394-a51e-5e49-9984-a7ac06825005" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478673} {:answer/id #uuid "56a8ed7c-03cd-521a-bb2f-1546988bfe36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478679} {:answer/id #uuid "742bbfa3-afc2-5d30-96a9-b323199b849c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478729} {:answer/id #uuid "7b4ebe60-62a8-5574-8302-183ff5c9dbe0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478742}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48f99d44-4d8f-5e63-a4f9-ad7e2a143699" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478663} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8dff0b2-a40e-5488-9228-977e8100365b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479029}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "854b6ede-01ab-595a-a1e4-df6af7f40d5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-15649" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{5}{8}\\sqrt{x}$ and the point $P(4,\\frac{5}{4})$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ determine the slope of $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ at the point $(\\frac{5}{4},4).$" :question/worked-solution "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(4,\\frac{5}{4}).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{5}{8}\\sqrt{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{5}{16\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{5}{32}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(4,\\frac{5}{4})$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(\\frac{5}{4},4)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(\\frac{5}{4}) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(\\frac{5}{4}))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{5}{32})} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{5}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479110 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dc9a6bd-a591-5999-b245-91b38db136b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478577} {:answer/id #uuid "6ed5ec33-0fac-5b1f-8846-71b9668a161a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478724} {:answer/id #uuid "a0981e4f-9bc7-5c90-b871-88aafbf5dc4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478820} {:answer/id #uuid "d358ce26-83c5-527a-b35c-94335a5980f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478894} {:answer/id #uuid "fcc58994-bee3-54d3-8345-6cff06c3be4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478954}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fcc58994-bee3-54d3-8345-6cff06c3be4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478954} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d527114-1572-54ac-b766-1707893768e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478995}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9ad4c6c3-50cf-5ba9-ae9f-35f5ce660c90" :question/math-academy-id "q-107497" :question/problem "Consider the LU decomposition of the matrix $A$ and the vector $b,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}}_{U},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf $⟨{x}_{1},{x}_{2}⟩$ and $⟨{y}_{1},{y}_{2}⟩$ are the solutions to the systems $Ax=b$ and $Ly=b$ respectively, what is the value of ${x}_{2}\\cdot {y}_{1}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,\\underbrace{(LU)}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL\\underbrace{(Ux)}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly &=b \\\\ Ux &=y.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the two systems in turn.\n\n- First, we solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=b=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &=1 \\\\ 9{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} &=6.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${y}_{1}=1$ and ${y}_{2}=-3.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\ny=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- Next, we solve $Ux=y.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=y=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=1 \\\\ 6{x}_{2} &=-3.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${x}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and ${x}_{1}=-2.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{2}\\cdot {y}_{1}=(-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot 1=-\\frac{1}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479111 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41f7a202-2c88-5780-a174-3331ee2546d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478649} {:answer/id #uuid "5b555cc0-0ed8-506e-80ce-b3b4bdb41696" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478686} {:answer/id #uuid "72872caa-7826-5d33-9478-418fcf7b7bc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478727} {:answer/id #uuid "968707ff-2d1c-5885-9528-97f8f79a7377" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478806} {:answer/id #uuid "dc5ffa82-9e00-5078-94cf-288a2d8f398a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478905}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41f7a202-2c88-5780-a174-3331ee2546d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478649} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb973e61-7d23-52e5-b2ef-12eefda53bf8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479050}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a26bf3d9-e722-517d-a584-8e9fe07e450e" :question/math-academy-id "q-186031" :question/problem "Evaluate $-6a-\\frac{1}{5}$ for $a=\\frac{1}{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "Replacing $a$ with $\\frac{1}{3}$ in the expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6a-\\frac{1}{5} &= -6(\\frac{1}{3})-\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ &= -\\frac{6}{3}-\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ &= -2-\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ &= \\frac{(-2)\\cdot 5-1}{5} \\\\ &= -\\frac{11}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479115 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85e8f87d-a86b-5e5a-9b4c-520af1d67778" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478767} {:answer/id #uuid "8a5de172-0879-5846-8775-50301538400b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478775} {:answer/id #uuid "8c404e33-978f-5492-8afd-92388f08864c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478781} {:answer/id #uuid "92a6058f-3718-55b4-83a2-afc873b42494" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478797} {:answer/id #uuid "a7862d49-f18d-5362-98d3-095ce5b8964a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{13}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478832}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7862d49-f18d-5362-98d3-095ce5b8964a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{13}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478832} :answer-field/id #uuid "63b35dd5-9730-5404-b881-f84b63c0e854" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478998}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ac78b028-e0aa-5c92-b54a-b1ba1527a82f" :question/math-academy-id "q-152915" :question/problem "A continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{4}(1-x),\\,-2\\le x\\le 0.\n$$\n\nCalculate $\\text{E}[{X}^{3}].$" :question/worked-solution "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the third raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{3}] & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}{x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}(1-x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}({x}^{3}-{x}^{4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}-\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}){|}_{-2}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(0-[4+\\frac{32}{5}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(-\\frac{52}{5}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{13}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479119 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b155602a-a9af-5cac-9ef9-d26df365210e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478845}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b155602a-a9af-5cac-9ef9-d26df365210e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478845} :answer-field/id #uuid "08cb8f7a-3eff-5e61-a8e7-a58164764082" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478962}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b47ad43a-e012-58c2-b33a-c151f7cd3d62" :question/math-academy-id "q-342577" :question/problem "![](images/37/37067c32385be4b652765062bbaf4e413ec87cec2ec6d0d01c6cd5dce64d7d9a.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a piecewise linear function $y=f(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(1,0)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $(0,1)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)?$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $(0,1)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(0)=1.$ \nThe slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ for $x\\in [-1,3]$ is $\\frac{3}{2}$, hence, ${f}^{′}(1)=\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is \n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479132 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "246e1b2f-6c69-5623-8546-f8f0f3b9f05b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478615} {:answer/id #uuid "2bdc9d8e-a8b0-5a1f-a275-4f9721c494f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478628} {:answer/id #uuid "3ebda5a4-ae46-5937-b31f-c78480be0131" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478647} {:answer/id #uuid "8f02771c-0941-5ad8-b645-f5b0bd475cd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478785} {:answer/id #uuid "b5ff3de9-2ac1-527c-8bea-2348693c73ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478853}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "246e1b2f-6c69-5623-8546-f8f0f3b9f05b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478615} :answer-field/id #uuid "525e14af-3d5d-5768-99db-cfa31e6ebfe1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478990}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d295fd4d-8093-5953-9c94-5f7f1a143168" :question/math-academy-id "q-110128" :question/problem "![](images/89/89dee9fffb22501189ac9f972dc4bcf7e2ed3602ef000b8ef01f04ddccd2e2eb.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a piecewise linear function $y=f(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(3,3)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $(3,3)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(3)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $(3,3)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(3)=3.$\nThe slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ for $x\\in [0,6]$ is $\\frac{1}{3}$, hence, ${f}^{′}(3)=\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(3) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(3))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479136 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10fe0189-99ac-5637-9392-a83123123131" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478581}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10fe0189-99ac-5637-9392-a83123123131" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478581} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6ce6b76-7d56-5c1e-ab35-c635323e2536" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479049}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e322fcd2-b2f5-5ca5-b94b-ad3cb0222a99" :question/math-academy-id "q-344912" :question/problem "![](images/e7/e78aafab1786a1fa7ee8e58c80c41b246179cfcce7399324731131f40e8d1490.png)\n\nGiven the plots of $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ above, find ${h}^{′}(-1)$ where $h(x)=\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.$\n\n${h}^{′}(-1)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The quotient rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)-f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x)}{(g(x))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(-1)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(-1)\\cdot g(-1)-f(-1)\\cdot {g}^{′}(-1)}{(g(-1))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graphs above, we obtain that $f(-1)=-1$ and $g(-1)=2.$\n\nAdditionally:\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (-3,1)$ equals $-1.$ So, ${f}^{′}(-1)=-1.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (-3,1)$ equals $\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, ${g}^{′}(-1)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(-1) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(-1)\\cdot g(-1)-f(-1)\\cdot {g}^{′}(-1)}{(g(-1))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})}{(2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\frac{3}{2}}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479831 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13ab2acd-ac6c-5c2a-995c-c1dee89cbd43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479388} {:answer/id #uuid "1909a1c9-6691-531d-93d0-870e5d5ef65a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479399} {:answer/id #uuid "2fbc6af7-9db2-57c1-834c-568900349313" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479433} {:answer/id #uuid "ac3c64de-c016-5a5b-8e71-f84fd4be4d25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479614} {:answer/id #uuid "df4336c2-5480-5d1a-a84d-7019f790df07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479688}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2fbc6af7-9db2-57c1-834c-568900349313" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479433} :answer-field/id #uuid "bde93567-ec99-5785-851e-83145a289d18" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479800}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0b5cb4d7-77e4-5ac4-8439-cb36ef6f1c98" :question/math-academy-id "q-6233" :question/problem "If $-\\frac{x}{7}=-3,$ then $x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's remove the negative sign from the variable. To do this, we multiply both sides of the equation by $-1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{x}{7} & =-3 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{7}) & =(-1)\\cdot (-3) \\\\ \\frac{x}{7} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the equation above, $x$ is being *divided* by $7.$ To isolate $x$, we can perform the opposite operation, which is *multiplying* by $7.$ So, we multiply both sides of the equation by $7$ and simplify.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{7} & =3 \\\\ 7\\cdot \\frac{x}{7} & =7\\cdot 3 \\\\ \\frac{7x}{7} & =21 \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{7}x}{\\cancel{7}} & =21 \\\\ x & =21\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479836 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "159fd380-dce4-518a-a6d3-698ca5bb3eba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{12.5}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479391} {:answer/id #uuid "40b810eb-accf-5a4e-97d9-c8605d6d85e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{108.4}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479454} {:answer/id #uuid "a6d164bc-18d8-5190-a58a-7a02287b7760" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{61.3}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479608} {:answer/id #uuid "cd55f423-f72d-545f-ae08-9223b18ea389" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{114.4}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479673} {:answer/id #uuid "e31d771e-69db-5724-9462-443d78f7ca71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{46.9}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479693}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40b810eb-accf-5a4e-97d9-c8605d6d85e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{108.4}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479454} :answer-field/id #uuid "30fc2b81-3bdf-59c5-9db1-77618c2dc14a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479760}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0ffc2b95-6d99-59f1-9d71-68fe0c7010aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-18321" :question/problem "Find the angle between $a=⟨1,-1,0⟩$ and $b=⟨0,1,-2⟩.$ Round your answer to $1$ decimal place." :question/worked-solution "Rearranging the dot product formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,a\\,||\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}+{a}_{z}^{2}}\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 0+(-1)\\cdot 1+0\\cdot (-2)}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}}\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}})≈{108.4}^{∘}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479850 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02cb6fee-ce7d-560e-8ed7-f39aa200d67d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{120}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479364} {:answer/id #uuid "1c69ef4d-faaf-5822-b026-b326887faa8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{135}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479404} {:answer/id #uuid "52500d10-896d-510b-8140-363810b9ef46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{150}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479478} {:answer/id #uuid "e01fccff-bbff-54ab-98f2-ea5e909b0798" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479690} {:answer/id #uuid "ebf08a51-4f87-56eb-a1bf-608bbb2ea4f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{180}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479708}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02cb6fee-ce7d-560e-8ed7-f39aa200d67d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{120}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479364} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2059258-9233-559a-842c-f610002a589b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479813}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4a507c40-e9a1-5cff-8a8b-72e01c23590f" :question/math-academy-id "q-69909" :question/problem "Find the angle between $a=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "Rearranging the dot product formula, we have: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,a\\,||\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}+{a}_{z}^{2}}\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 0+1\\cdot (-1)+0\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2})={120}^{∘}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479853 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "632ae4e3-ee1f-5e8a-90cc-3116a07be361" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2u}{v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479505} {:answer/id #uuid "6c54a42e-7de5-5fb7-b8c8-7174b039dd83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{u}{2v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479523} {:answer/id #uuid "83b435cc-8b1e-5f1c-8aa8-a11fa47c493e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2uv}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479552} {:answer/id #uuid "b019fb8d-4639-5879-8f60-c5aaf51eda53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{v}{2u}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479622} {:answer/id #uuid "c815be35-c8ac-52ba-8f94-4f8209e87970" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2v}{u}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479662}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83b435cc-8b1e-5f1c-8aa8-a11fa47c493e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2uv}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479552} :answer-field/id #uuid "4670dc2b-4eab-5b4e-ad02-4eea217ed995" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479767}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "52539dbe-37de-5500-97cf-839689e21baa" :question/math-academy-id "q-160585" :question/problem "Consider all non-critical points of the transformation $T:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y)\\to (u(x,y),v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} \\\\ x+y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T$ in terms of $u$ and $v.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y)={e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ and $v(x,y)=x+y.$ \n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =2x{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =2y{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2x{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} & -2y{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2x{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\cdot 1-(-2y{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2x{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}+2y{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (x+y)\\cdot {e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =2uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2uv}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479875 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05d03192-075e-55e0-bdfc-5e2f0694c8cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{76.23}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479367} {:answer/id #uuid "4fa9dd91-9cae-5db7-9b32-261b4c37d3a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{79.11}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479475} {:answer/id #uuid "7f2d3fee-96ee-53a9-be0e-e1fbe16389df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{62.32}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479546} {:answer/id #uuid "a5f880ae-7e88-52b7-a206-237ab0ac051c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{64.66}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479607} {:answer/id #uuid "e0f37a48-fcb8-529a-828b-a0f326d438e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{75.31}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479691}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0f37a48-fcb8-529a-828b-a0f326d438e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{75.31}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479691} :answer-field/id #uuid "0dc4cf74-ec7a-5064-a626-43941e3de50c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479746}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9369c09e-6bcc-5680-9471-b4a602dad377" :question/math-academy-id "q-140568" :question/problem "Rounded to two decimal places, what is the acute angle between the vectors $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $w=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}?$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{v\\cdot w}{‖v‖\\,‖w‖},\n$$\n\nwe have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{{v}_{1}{w}_{1}+{v}_{2}{w}_{2}+{v}_{3}{w}_{3}+{v}_{4}{w}_{4}}{\\sqrt{{v}_{1}^{2}+{v}_{2}^{2}+{v}_{3}^{2}+{v}_{4}^{2}}\\,\\sqrt{{w}_{1}^{2}+{w}_{2}^{2}+{w}_{3}^{2}+{w}_{4}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 3+(-1)\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 0}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\,\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}\\cdot \\sqrt{14}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{140}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{35}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{35}})≈{75.31}^{∘}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479876 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ed77687-b07d-532f-8400-2a71d4a5ad46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479380} {:answer/id #uuid "74be835c-e26b-5172-ac8b-75460bf573b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479537} {:answer/id #uuid "8e9ac26e-5d8d-5f86-b225-32abc20a36ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479573} {:answer/id #uuid "c5a561f8-22fa-5008-be18-18d377fa405a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479658} {:answer/id #uuid "caa5e437-184f-52d9-8cfa-224935e09da8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479667}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e9ac26e-5d8d-5f86-b225-32abc20a36ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479573} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab9b2392-4e70-5610-9864-41016bfde623" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479796}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "99c3ab32-c332-5aab-98f3-193b3fead123" :question/math-academy-id "q-167500" :question/problem "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy+z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2},\\frac{1}{t},t⟩$ at the point $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy+z)),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy+z)),\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy+z))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy+z),-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy+z),-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy+z)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={t}^{2},y=\\frac{1}{t},$ and $z=t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨-\\frac{1}{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),-{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),-{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩\\cdot ⟨2t,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},1⟩ \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\cdot 2t+(-{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}})+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))] & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479882 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b5bfd06-95e1-5941-b827-ceeac852f83e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479375} {:answer/id #uuid "3256080f-70d0-5fb8-a894-8e796e21fcea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479437} {:answer/id #uuid "4c88a484-7f77-57a9-b38b-ae25dd456160" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479473} {:answer/id #uuid "55dcc1fb-ebd9-55f7-95ad-86544ff060eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479480} {:answer/id #uuid "60abafac-8bc5-5541-a465-e3ddc61858bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479501}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60abafac-8bc5-5541-a465-e3ddc61858bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479501} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8961cfc-c61f-5d7e-9ac8-9ae2e5f043be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479809}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b4c0e9de-946c-5a43-9beb-5626829b00ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-75388" :question/problem "If $-\\frac{c}{12}=4,$ then $c=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's remove the negative sign from the variable. To do this, we multiply both sides of the equation by $-1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{c}{12} & =4 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-\\frac{c}{12}) & =(-1)\\cdot 4 \\\\ \\frac{c}{12} & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the equation above, $c$ is being divided by $12.$ To isolate $c$, we can perform the opposite operation, which is *multiplying* by $12.$ So, we multiply both sides of the equation by $12$ and simplify.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{c}{12} & =-4 \\\\ 12\\cdot \\frac{c}{12} & =12\\cdot (-4) \\\\ \\frac{12c}{12} & =-48 \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{12}c}{\\cancel{12}} & =-48 \\\\ c & =-48\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479887 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1eba0eb3-b779-54f6-8b71-69c87e7e162f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479411} {:answer/id #uuid "6660c89c-395f-5a54-bd86-8fd6ca2f7aa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479512} {:answer/id #uuid "6e3af763-2209-577b-a66e-d0ff71895b50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479524} {:answer/id #uuid "8ebd8e6e-e81d-5280-9397-ef529ebc5507" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479574} {:answer/id #uuid "bb49af1f-b29f-550f-9cde-bf9edc9236ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479641}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e3af763-2209-577b-a66e-d0ff71895b50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479524} :answer-field/id #uuid "75c56a84-e3bc-5ebd-9369-ca60eb86d427" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479782}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bfba5122-2e77-55a1-99f5-47e064f8cc14" :question/math-academy-id "q-69437" :question/problem "${(\\frac{7}{8})}^{-1}=$" :question/worked-solution "The expression ${(\\frac{7}{8})}^{-1}$ means the reciprocal of $\\frac{7}{8}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{7}{8})}^{-1}=\\frac{8}{7}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479895 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e96ef40-f7b4-505a-b88a-506d83db1f61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479409} {:answer/id #uuid "3072be43-dcf8-5fa8-9494-99e39ee2dd94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479435} {:answer/id #uuid "340b3cb9-ad67-517b-a6e0-f0d7db397ded" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479439} {:answer/id #uuid "8f368bc1-aef0-57e8-b86a-54eae8aa0cd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479575} {:answer/id #uuid "ce4590ae-7be8-50df-a6d0-f5cac7ad9ebe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479675}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f368bc1-aef0-57e8-b86a-54eae8aa0cd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479575} :answer-field/id #uuid "53643ac3-21d5-5bf8-855c-7a10edbfc067" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479771}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "da68c3e8-bcc9-5b44-b44b-1f5e2fc2eb9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-71157" :question/problem "${(\\frac{2}{11})}^{-1}=$" :question/worked-solution "The expression ${(\\frac{2}{11})}^{-1}$ means the reciprocal of $\\frac{2}{11}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{2}{11})}^{-1}=\\frac{11}{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479989 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a9d9676-be2e-55f1-8608-d21cf8ae6b8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}}{5} + \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)} + 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479944} {:answer/id #uuid "098d7e1e-212e-4a5b-bfd2-23d0455e3560" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\ln5}{5}+\\ln2+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569322}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a9d9676-be2e-55f1-8608-d21cf8ae6b8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}}{5} + \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)} + 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479944} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6f7efb5-5352-5998-97f1-7e7dd561f8f9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479984}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cf41a068-fd0d-5f4b-9f3b-52ecb794226b" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=1\\cdot {5}^{x}+2\\cdot {2}^{x}-3x^2+1$, find the instantaneous rate of change at $x=-1.$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Applying the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f'(x)&=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1\\cdot {5}^{x}+2\\cdot {2}^{x}-3x^2+1) \\\\ &=1\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({5}^{x})+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({2}^{x})-3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x^2)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1) \\\\ &=1({5}^{x}\\ln(5))+2({2}^{x}\\ln(2))-3(2x)+0 \\\\ &=1\\ln(5)\\cdot {5}^{x}+2\\ln(2)\\cdot {2}^{x}-6x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change at $x=-1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f'(-1)&=1\\ln(5)\\cdot {5}^{(-1)}+2\\ln(2)\\cdot {2}^{(-1)}-6(-1) \\\\ &=1(\\frac{1}{5})\\ln(5)+2(\\frac{1}{2})\\ln(2)-(6)(-1) \\\\ &=\\frac{\\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}}{5} + \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)} + 6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479996 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b6645ee-3c3d-53ef-9146-60dda2be4d17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}}{3} + \\frac{\\ln{\\left(6 \\right)}}{3} + 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479958}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b6645ee-3c3d-53ef-9146-60dda2be4d17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}}{3} + \\frac{\\ln{\\left(6 \\right)}}{3} + 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479958} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d4c7dd4-b186-55cd-b20f-586c239c7e13" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479970}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f8120d99-4d4d-56ef-af5e-c92f6fe86369" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=2\\cdot {6}^{x}+1\\cdot {3}^{x}-3x^2+2$, find the instantaneous rate of change at $x=-1.$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Applying the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f'(x)&=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\cdot {6}^{x}+1\\cdot {3}^{x}-3x^2+2) \\\\ &=2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({6}^{x})+1\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({3}^{x})-3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x^2)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2) \\\\ &=2({6}^{x}\\ln(6))+1({3}^{x}\\ln(3))-3(2x)+0 \\\\ &=2\\ln(6)\\cdot {6}^{x}+1\\ln(3)\\cdot {3}^{x}-6x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change at $x=-1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f'(-1)&=2\\ln(6)\\cdot {6}^{(-1)}+1\\ln(3)\\cdot {3}^{(-1)}-6(-1) \\\\ &=2(\\frac{1}{6})\\ln(6)+1(\\frac{1}{3})\\ln(3)-(6)(-1) \\\\ &=\\frac{\\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}}{3} + \\frac{\\ln{\\left(6 \\right)}}{3} + 6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480635 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40a24c23-247d-5712-9a28-a78576688a6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480217}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40a24c23-247d-5712-9a28-a78576688a6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480217} :answer-field/id #uuid "e83883fd-0df1-5652-851f-22e92beb519e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480615}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0c468631-3494-5a5c-8263-59a9e6ef38aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-185312" :question/problem "Expressed as a fraction in its simplest form, ${9}^{-5}\\times {9}^{3}=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To multiply powers of the same base, we add the exponents:\n\n$$\n{9}^{-5}\\times {9}^{3}={9}^{-5+3}={9}^{-2}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate ${9}^{-2},$ we find the reciprocal of the base $(9)$ and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the opposite sign:\n\n$$\n{9}^{-2}={(\\frac{1}{9})}^{2}=\\frac{1}{9}\\times \\frac{1}{9}=\\frac{1}{81}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480654 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2638936c-5b0f-58a4-994e-0d8105ed344e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{(\\frac{6}{5})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480172} {:answer/id #uuid "3635da9f-6e9e-5c6c-b0df-8b6493466f48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{5}{6})}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480198} {:answer/id #uuid "7ce93af7-3222-5d51-a2a5-1849488558cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{6}{5})}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480316} {:answer/id #uuid "83290b9d-c95c-5ea2-a4b2-df6ea439c113" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480329} {:answer/id #uuid "c29dd532-663b-5c61-a8e3-572bc60ac813" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{(\\frac{5}{6})}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480435}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3635da9f-6e9e-5c6c-b0df-8b6493466f48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{5}{6})}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480198} :answer-field/id #uuid "f528d313-aed4-573c-9b07-3ba92a86a40f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480623}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4de46f6f-1e6b-5ab8-947e-dc550a3db276" :question/math-academy-id "q-128737" :question/problem "Express $\\frac{36}{25}$ as a base raised to a negative exponent." :question/worked-solution "First, we write $\\frac{36}{25}$ as a base raised to a power:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{36}{25} & =\\frac{6\\times 6}{5\\times 5} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\times \\frac{6}{5} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{6}{5})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the reciprocal of the base $(\\frac{6}{5}),$ and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the *opposite* sign $(-2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{6}{5})}^{2}={(\\frac{5}{6})}^{-2}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480657 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba2f1956-9c7f-5c6f-b22c-f0da7d94a10f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480420}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba2f1956-9c7f-5c6f-b22c-f0da7d94a10f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480420} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c042d8d-57b0-5cf2-82ee-696d9e75dc07" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480559}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "52bff138-3308-56fb-8e00-4b3a80e44a0d" :question/math-academy-id "q-173748" :question/problem "Expressed as a whole number or fraction in its simplest form, ${(\\frac{1}{10})}^{-3}=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We find the reciprocal of the base $(\\frac{1}{10}),$ and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the *opposite* sign $(+3),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{-3} & =(10)^{3} \\\\  & =10\\cdot 10\\cdot 10 \\\\  & =1,000\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480675 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "752a3ed9-4c29-5165-ac74-445a7667e23c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480300}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "752a3ed9-4c29-5165-ac74-445a7667e23c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480300} :answer-field/id #uuid "539ad74a-5113-59bb-99ce-f5b195bb6b58" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480558}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "967d4b0f-c5b8-576b-b414-bb183f8a1110" :question/math-academy-id "q-343368" :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-3, & x\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x, & x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ converges when $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{+}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is not continuous at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ The one-sided limits at this point are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & ={lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0 \\\\ {f}^{+}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & ={lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}f(x)=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{-}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+{f}^{+}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{2}=\\frac{0+(-3)}{2}=-\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480676 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b823059-32a5-5517-a7b7-fb48a969e9a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480241}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b823059-32a5-5517-a7b7-fb48a969e9a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480241} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c112df9-705c-527b-8556-06919619c93f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480568}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "990eaa9e-cd8b-5349-b27e-24596fc286e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-353469" :question/problem "The number $y(t)$ of homes in a city that have adopted clean energy follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3y(20-\\frac{y}{125}),\\,y(0)=100,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years, since the observations began. According to this model, what is the maximum possible number of homes that will adopt clean energy?\n\nThe maximum possible number of homes that will adopt clean energy is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe carrying capacity $a$ represents the maximum possible number of clean-energy-adopting homes in the city. A diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](images/d8/d8133f1c4203903bac606bbae61c73717e2ba2dc15a97a77f48eaaeaf4736d06.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3y(20-\\frac{y}{125}) \\\\  & =3y(\\frac{2\\,500-y}{125}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3y}{125}(2\\,500-y) \\\\  & =0.024y(2\\,500-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=2500.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $2500,$ and we conclude that the maximum possible number of homes that will adopt clean energy is $2500.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480688 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4768dc17-128a-5264-bb20-23540bc93f6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}p" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480227} {:answer/id #uuid "66271f18-bf53-53fa-83e6-4aaa92faf9f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-p" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480278} {:answer/id #uuid "a7828ce3-5e74-5c71-b0fe-85a96d7ec54d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2p" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480391} {:answer/id #uuid "b0ca58b2-3b12-5d53-ad1b-c58a6e468632" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2p" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480407} {:answer/id #uuid "b6b21f9d-12ea-536b-b50b-3098e10aa880" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480416}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0ca58b2-3b12-5d53-ad1b-c58a6e468632" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2p" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480407} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9c3dd9f-ab18-5360-9b3b-eadcd53e98f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480600}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b6749b1c-5bbb-5e2e-a55f-6596daa98287" :question/math-academy-id "q-126505" :question/problem "Suppose that $|\\,p\\,|=3$, $|\\,q\\,|=6\\sqrt{2}$ and that the angle between the vectors is $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$ Find the vector projection of $q$ onto $p$ in terms of the vector $p.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the scalar projection as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{p}\\,q & =|\\,q\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the unit vector that is parallel to $p:$\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{p}{|\\,p\\,|}=\\frac{p}{3}=\\frac{1}{3}p\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute the vector projection as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{p}\\,q & ={\\text{comp}}_{p}\\,q\\cdot u \\\\  & =-6\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}p \\\\  & =-2p\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480693 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3006a724-b763-5eca-a3b5-b0cae30c9023" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{256}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480189} {:answer/id #uuid "d02f1926-f911-4a0f-9d73-a06532389950" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186618092}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3006a724-b763-5eca-a3b5-b0cae30c9023" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{256}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480189} :answer-field/id #uuid "68579504-4350-520d-a0e5-3de9612d9cff" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480566}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c75ad058-938d-576a-bc4e-215aa43fed14" :question/math-academy-id "q-175985" :question/problem "Expressed as a fraction in its simplest form, ${4}^{-4}=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We find the reciprocal of the base $(4),$ and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the *opposite* sign $(+4),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{-4} & ={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{4} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{4})\\times (\\frac{1}{4})\\times (\\frac{1}{4})\\times (\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1\\times 1\\times 1\\times 1}{4\\times 4\\times 4\\times 4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{256}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480697 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "081d2611-c9d5-5ff8-acf0-23903af25f7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480126} {:answer/id #uuid "2cb6f314-795c-5293-badb-a09dd380b034" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480182} {:answer/id #uuid "4004a3d0-2cbf-51cf-a3c3-743093b7b616" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480215} {:answer/id #uuid "7ac27f65-90ba-5e1a-903c-2209418975d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "900" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480313} {:answer/id #uuid "a7813003-46c8-549a-951a-80403b679f4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480390}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "081d2611-c9d5-5ff8-acf0-23903af25f7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480126} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9c885db-a51b-565f-802d-b5e3e3429fb9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480601}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "da1f1140-0b6f-5e8e-98c1-c5bc7c260a47" :question/math-academy-id "q-118587" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation for the function $y=y(t):$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=9y(1-\\frac{y}{1\\,200}),\\,y(0)=200\n$$\n\nWhat is ${lim}_{t\\to \\infty }y(t)?$" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nA diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](images/57/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =9y(1-\\frac{y}{1\\,200}) \\\\  & =9y(\\frac{1\\,200-y}{1\\,200}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9y}{1\\,200}(1\\,200-y) \\\\  & =\\frac{3y}{400}(1\\,200-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=1\\,200.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $1\\,200.$\n\nFor a given logistic growth model, $y\\to a$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to \\infty }y(t)=1\\,200.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480708 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4cd74255-cb9f-5d8c-835b-d4b5716f3ea4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480242} {:answer/id #uuid "58dafc0d-d970-5fdf-bd4d-323f6d360781" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480260} {:answer/id #uuid "98d7d377-a710-5f5a-88d4-bc89948036ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480363} {:answer/id #uuid "bc4b3278-a327-5d85-aa9d-e9d61be3a1c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480423} {:answer/id #uuid "d4754cd8-29db-546d-bbf6-c0e4fedfb324" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480470}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4cd74255-cb9f-5d8c-835b-d4b5716f3ea4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480242} :answer-field/id #uuid "984a702d-477d-57e0-aacd-910dcf28c310" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480587}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f6527ad8-2bed-55da-aea0-3bbb3266c020" :question/math-academy-id "q-6197" :question/problem "${4}^{-3}=$" :question/worked-solution "We find the reciprocal of the base $(4),$ and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the *opposite* sign $(+3),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{-3} & ={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{3} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{4})\\times (\\frac{1}{4})\\times (\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1\\times 1\\times 1}{4\\times 4\\times 4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{64}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480710 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e26fae8-12e8-52a5-b1e8-819d8831696d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{40}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480211} {:answer/id #uuid "62a5cb40-626b-5d02-b703-7a92b71cd317" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480272} {:answer/id #uuid "65a95b74-54a8-51ba-8a8f-b8f04d17ce65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{1}{36})}^{40}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480275} {:answer/id #uuid "95a368b3-c5a4-586f-ab3d-ce069e94a660" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480357} {:answer/id #uuid "f2446c34-bd64-5b92-966a-21f89e99be19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480508}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2446c34-bd64-5b92-966a-21f89e99be19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480508} :answer-field/id #uuid "e567ac44-1393-5520-adf0-f9ec118ed8bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480612}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fa0bbd4b-06ea-5732-8998-73cc5cb59649" :question/math-academy-id "q-68303" :question/problem "${(\\frac{1}{6})}^{5}\\times {(\\frac{1}{6})}^{8}=$" :question/worked-solution "To multiply powers of the same base, we add the exponents:\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{5}\\times {(\\frac{1}{6})}^{8}={(\\frac{1}{6})}^{5+8}={(\\frac{1}{6})}^{13}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481343 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30fd4f57-3f0a-5ebc-aa07-bb84afb9cc7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480833} {:answer/id #uuid "3f270f12-02ec-5549-8988-b03e5d2eae95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480861} {:answer/id #uuid "cc1e541a-a0b5-50e1-8a46-d0f83df1d6c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481124} {:answer/id #uuid "e902cbe7-031f-5540-a22a-6e30754fbd91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481184} {:answer/id #uuid "fbe6534a-9156-5f3a-a3f4-adabfe81028f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481226}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f270f12-02ec-5549-8988-b03e5d2eae95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480861} :answer-field/id #uuid "149fcd69-9998-58e5-a043-dbc9ef09dd8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481248}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "03e70789-b720-5486-ac24-d7c0b4013554" :question/math-academy-id "q-160937" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{x+3y}{x}\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the quadrilateral region with vertices $(1,-\\frac{1}{3}),$  $(2,-\\frac{2}{3}),$  $(2,-\\frac{1}{3}),$ and $(1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{x+3y}{x}\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v=x+3y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(1,-\\frac{1}{3}) & =(1,1+3(-\\frac{1}{3})) \\\\  & =(1,0) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(2,-\\frac{2}{3}) & =(2,2+3(-\\frac{2}{3})) \\\\  & =(2,0) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(2,-\\frac{1}{3}) & =(2,2+3(-\\frac{1}{3})) \\\\  & =(2,1) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(1,0) & =(1,1+3(0)) \\\\  & =(1,1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/33/33a7fa05505942d0729e5a5be2bd691a49c242937fdb9de18f6a6f97b5799424.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{x+3y}{x}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{v}{u}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{v}{u}\\,|\\frac{1}{3}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{v}{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}v\\,\\text{d}v{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\cdot [\\operatorname{ln}⁡u]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481348 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "069138e9-203c-5336-a303-692fb1a04b6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480750} {:answer/id #uuid "3be8edd8-c7ec-5754-a981-50e1d2d61749" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480854} {:answer/id #uuid "b2d41d76-c8a4-5085-9df7-04c8d4913cd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481076} {:answer/id #uuid "c33b5db7-e254-5fd2-94ab-40b1cb417d41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481115} {:answer/id #uuid "f1625dec-f23d-5eb0-987c-e4d1977364f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481206}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2d41d76-c8a4-5085-9df7-04c8d4913cd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481076} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d3f6daa-40ec-59fb-8ee1-def277ce39d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481245}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0fd9a9b1-ea5c-5d82-ac2e-6cadd4a02789" :question/math-academy-id "q-163234" :question/problem "Find the average value of the function $f(x,y)=6xy$ on the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2{y}^{2},\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}$ given that the area of the region is $\\frac{2}{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "Applying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{ave}} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{2}{3})}{∬}_{D}6xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2{y}^{2}}6xy\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[3{x}^{2}y]_{x=0}^{x=2{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}12{y}^{5}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}[2{y}^{6}]_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(2-0) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481353 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff37ba3f-b657-5468-a046-c7a885ae86ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481235}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff37ba3f-b657-5468-a046-c7a885ae86ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481235} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9a275f8-e12f-531b-9ed6-2c3eda31cf0e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481313}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2dbc86c1-eb39-5436-87d8-eb16a5d4d270" :question/math-academy-id "q-179401" :question/problem "$(7-5)^{-4}+{(\\frac{3}{5}-\\frac{1}{2})}^{0}=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS. \n\nFirst, we compute the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(7-5)^{-4}+{(\\frac{3}{5}-\\frac{1}{2})}^{0} &= {2}^{-4}+{(\\frac{6}{10}-\\frac{5}{10})}^{0} \\\\ &= {2}^{-4}+{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that ${(\\frac{1}{10})}^{0}=1.$ So, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{-4}+{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{0} &= {2}^{-4}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, notice that ${2}^{-4}=\\frac{1}{{2}^{4}}=\\frac{1}{16}.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{-4}+1 &= \\frac{1}{16}+1 \\\\ &= \\frac{1+16}{16} \\\\ &= \\frac{17}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481356 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2af634ac-2adf-5266-9d5e-f4ec6782ebb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480817} {:answer/id #uuid "6a7caeaf-072f-52ba-8fa7-2bd619dc7e84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480937} {:answer/id #uuid "b3cc978a-23f8-5e0c-a899-6cecd8ec9666" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481082} {:answer/id #uuid "c9ba0f1e-64d5-5b98-bd5a-3378ee864a41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481121} {:answer/id #uuid "e4fc9398-1cd8-5d45-bef7-2d3eb160e0af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481174}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2af634ac-2adf-5266-9d5e-f4ec6782ebb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480817} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f12123e-d897-5bb5-a021-652c5ffc786d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481283}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "379e6627-cf03-580e-9f81-1437be9f7987" :question/math-academy-id "q-110978" :question/problem "Consider the matrix given by $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.$ Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $b=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 12 \\\\ k+30\\end{bmatrix}$ belongs to $\\text{Col}(A).$" :question/worked-solution "If $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(A),$ then the equation $Ax=b$ must be consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[A\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,b] & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 5 & k+30\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 12 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & k+30\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 21 \\\\ 0 & 5 & k+30\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=(\\frac{1}{3}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 5 & k+30\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-5{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k-5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=12 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=7 \\\\ 0 &=k-5.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn order for this system to be consistent, we require $0=k-5,$ which means that $k=5.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481363 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16f8819e-1e45-5b70-843f-9474e62016e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480782} {:answer/id #uuid "2dcb854a-8359-5e93-87cf-89bffc23af38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480823} {:answer/id #uuid "30e0bd06-5ba4-5322-8aaa-c8efbd6bb22c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480832} {:answer/id #uuid "54604561-d490-5cf0-8396-166d4506693f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480890} {:answer/id #uuid "8567ab33-79b0-5c93-ab46-2e6895393cb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480994}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16f8819e-1e45-5b70-843f-9474e62016e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480782} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1c3af90-4b4e-5817-986c-ad8b162022f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481335}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4b9728bb-13e5-5813-917f-2ed0c37ac7bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-86847" :question/problem "$$\nV=\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $V,$ given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{c}{a}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote \n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{(-1)\\cdot (-3)+0\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 2}{(-3)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, we have $a=\\frac{1}{2},$ $c=1,$ and \n\n$$\n\\frac{c}{a}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})}=2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481364 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09a70e77-e143-5fe1-987f-6cf3d9622cb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480759} {:answer/id #uuid "16926c3a-001d-572a-beb5-ac09cba7435e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480780} {:answer/id #uuid "930bb660-443d-5920-8d2a-07cfdc6d0a50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481014} {:answer/id #uuid "943844b2-6990-576d-9141-b10699c96298" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481018} {:answer/id #uuid "96c98676-4b18-52e8-baf0-b525847f1830" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481029}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "930bb660-443d-5920-8d2a-07cfdc6d0a50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481014} :answer-field/id #uuid "1dca1b12-338a-5c5f-badf-6213000ef5a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481249}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4d5d128b-3cee-5601-8961-cb121a4bf681" :question/math-academy-id "q-160903" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{(x-y)^{2}}\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the quadrilateral region with vertices $(1,0),$ $(2,1),$ $(3,0),$ and $(2,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{(x-y)^{2}}=\\frac{x+y}{x-y}\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=x-y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(1,0) & =(1+0,1-0) \\\\  & =(1,1) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(2,1) & =(2+1,2-1) \\\\  & =(3,1) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(3,0) & =(3+0,3-0) \\\\  & =(3,3) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(2,-1) & =(2+(-1),2-(-1)) \\\\  & =(1,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,1\\le v\\le 3\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/f3/f34926c196b850fa22e0f918b27c8d25e5deb7f6aa7ec232e2eeaf4e46dd309c.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{(x-y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x+y}{x-y}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}\\,|-\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{u}{v}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\text{d}v{\\int }_{1}^{3}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {[\\operatorname{ln}⁡v]}_{1}^{3}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481368 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e4ba564-c701-5756-89b9-c57ee635a3fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{4}+{x}^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480977} {:answer/id #uuid "9939d3e9-7f4e-5cc1-9753-721ee0a8d618" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}-2{x}^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481032} {:answer/id #uuid "cf656d9b-75ef-554a-9036-89079b6ca3d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{4}-2{x}^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481126} {:answer/id #uuid "e6b65738-d03d-56a9-a07a-8e4b68fc5c09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481180} {:answer/id #uuid "eccfdde5-d210-5907-b9d1-5dcc7cc45b70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{4}+{x}^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481195}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf656d9b-75ef-554a-9036-89079b6ca3d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{4}-2{x}^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481126} :answer-field/id #uuid "711cfcc8-2acd-5237-b383-a491ba95980c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481277}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "526966d4-b472-58c0-be00-2ffec30b650e" :question/math-academy-id "q-74584" :question/problem "$6{x}^{3}(\\frac{x}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})=$" :question/worked-solution "We multiply each term inside the parentheses by the term outside the parentheses, simplify using the rules of exponents, and then we add the results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{3}(\\frac{x}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}) &= (6{x}^{3})(\\frac{x}{2})+(6{x}^{3})(-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}) \\\\ &= (6{x}^{3})(\\frac{1}{2}x)+(6{x}^{3})(-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}) \\\\ &= (6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2})({x}^{3}\\cdot x)+(6\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3}))({x}^{3}\\cdot {x}^{3}) \\\\ &= (3)({x}^{3+1})+(-2)\\cdot ({x}^{3+3}) \\\\ &= 3{x}^{4}-2{x}^{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481371 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f4c86f2-5cfb-5693-a142-edc6be59008a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480767} {:answer/id #uuid "344eed9f-15e2-5d9d-a328-322b0adeb917" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{ln}^{3}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480839} {:answer/id #uuid "68632396-ccfe-581e-8589-6ce419497b94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{ln}^{3}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480933} {:answer/id #uuid "79ad7697-010f-568d-851d-4911a16f499a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{ln}^{2}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480962} {:answer/id #uuid "95eca681-53e5-51f4-8569-95f9733c2dbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{ln}^{2}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481024}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68632396-ccfe-581e-8589-6ce419497b94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{ln}^{3}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480933} :answer-field/id #uuid "f081c215-96de-53ac-b301-926b90ac00ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481333}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5de90e63-3afe-527a-b599-3cdcd703aa52" :question/math-academy-id "q-83656" :question/problem "Using the change of variables $u={e}^{x}$ and $v={e}^{y},$ evaluate the double integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}y\\,dx\\,dy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le {e}^{x}\\le 2,\\,1\\le {e}^{y}\\le 4\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu={e}^{x},\\,v={e}^{y}\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,1\\le v\\le 4\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{x} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\text{ }\\text{ }{e}^{y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{x+y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{{e}^{x+y}}=\\frac{1}{uv}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{ln}⁡v\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{ln}⁡v\\cdot |\\frac{1}{uv}|\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}[{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡v}{v}\\,dv]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}{[\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡v}{2}]}_{1}^{4}du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}[{ln}^{2}⁡4-{ln}^{2}⁡1]\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡4}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\,du \\\\  & =2{ln}^{2}⁡2[\\operatorname{ln}⁡u]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =2{ln}^{2}⁡2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1) \\\\  & =2{ln}^{3}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481373 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f5e6d20-6d83-5a4b-9919-511354ddc37f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-87" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480828} {:answer/id #uuid "76d69649-f0c6-5771-848d-71954873581b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "87" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480957} {:answer/id #uuid "b381b4b1-c1bc-52ab-8b7e-21ef5860d874" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-89" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481079} {:answer/id #uuid "e6602306-1f53-57b5-91c7-8b39e35659a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "89" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481178} {:answer/id #uuid "fdeff659-436b-5f34-ac81-a6409e40fce1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-121" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481230}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f5e6d20-6d83-5a4b-9919-511354ddc37f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-87" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480828} :answer-field/id #uuid "93d546fe-e93e-5d3c-8f24-4f640019211e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481291}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5e5e796d-b724-597d-a614-e8af03095fdf" :question/math-academy-id "q-100301" :question/problem "Calculate ${(\\frac{8}{7}-\\frac{1}{16})}^{0}-11\\times {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{-3}.$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nFirst, notice that\n$\\frac{8}{7}-\\frac{1}{16}$\nis not zero. Any non-zero number raised to the power of $0$ equals $1.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n{(\\frac{8}{7}-\\frac{1}{16})}^{0}=1.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the negative exponent and get\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{-3}={(\\frac{2}{1})}^{3}={2}^{3}=8.\n$$\n\nSubstituting all these values into the expression, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{8}{7}-\\frac{1}{16})}^{0}-11\\times {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{-3} &= 1-11\\times 8 \\\\ &= 1-88 \\\\ &= -87\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481384 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0321eccf-a272-5f41-9dc6-3afd94e414aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{6}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480743} {:answer/id #uuid "2eb956e4-324e-5b0c-8781-18bffb7582ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480826} {:answer/id #uuid "a507fac6-cc7c-5ccd-80d1-92dffbbe2f70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{1}{12})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481049} {:answer/id #uuid "eaf1341a-cc4c-5d47-be72-ffec29422a3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{12}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481192} {:answer/id #uuid "f7f53762-c988-521c-bc03-a77b4ff640a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{6}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481217}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0321eccf-a272-5f41-9dc6-3afd94e414aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{6}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480743} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e81aa8f-4f0c-580e-8d47-2566380e9716" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481255}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "747c9c25-846d-5f90-8282-9333af5562e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-68148" :question/problem "${({(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2})}^{-2}=$" :question/worked-solution "To raise a power to another power, we simply multiply the exponents:\n\n$$\n{({(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2})}^{-2}={(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2\\cdot (-2)}={(\\frac{1}{6})}^{-4}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate ${(\\frac{1}{6})}^{-4},$ we find the reciprocal of the base $\\frac{1}{6}$ and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the opposite sign:\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{-4}={6}^{4}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481386 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01c03964-bbb3-565c-bfcb-c6ed69ba5e55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{9}^{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480739} {:answer/id #uuid "2150377d-9170-5e26-8c05-6932a47baf31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{1}{9})}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480806} {:answer/id #uuid "78a5e0b0-0aff-56f5-b302-e96894a6e57c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{1}{9})}^{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480961} {:answer/id #uuid "8ff6cccd-26e2-5560-99d7-944b84007b58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{9}^{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481010} {:answer/id #uuid "96a52254-ea30-53d5-b2d5-8fa3a1d84466" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{1}{9})}^{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481028}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96a52254-ea30-53d5-b2d5-8fa3a1d84466" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{1}{9})}^{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481028} :answer-field/id #uuid "de337785-0bed-5d88-927f-c04df51fff3b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481325}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "77363ffd-e93f-5e69-9243-0a270550e6fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-100392" :question/problem "${({9}^{3})}^{-6}=$" :question/worked-solution "To raise a power to another power, we simply multiply the exponents:\n\n$$\n{({9}^{3})}^{-6}={9}^{3\\cdot (-6)}={9}^{-18}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate ${9}^{-18},$ we find the reciprocal of the base $(9)$ and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the opposite sign:\n\n$$\n{9}^{-18}={(\\frac{1}{9})}^{18}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481387 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48a6250d-baed-5bdc-8c18-ec991f18cc76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480876} {:answer/id #uuid "66b67c8f-ddbd-578f-9fb4-00cea9df2f02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{-24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480927} {:answer/id #uuid "95f9b5fa-1ab3-5fca-b270-eac093aeaeaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481025} {:answer/id #uuid "c036d25a-3226-544b-a887-2949559d9959" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{-10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481106} {:answer/id #uuid "f75cb532-ba73-5518-ab8f-23060fc5755d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481215}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66b67c8f-ddbd-578f-9fb4-00cea9df2f02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{-24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480927} :answer-field/id #uuid "d837b96a-1bb5-5d45-9d90-c7e7ac4028ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481320}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7c245f72-80de-502f-91e6-10b3d05c7ce1" :question/math-academy-id "q-141303" :question/problem "${({(\\frac{4}{5})}^{-4})}^{6}=$" :question/worked-solution "To raise a power to another power, we simply multiply the exponents:\n\n$$\n{({(\\frac{4}{5})}^{-4})}^{6}={(\\frac{4}{5})}^{(-4)\\cdot 6}={(\\frac{4}{5})}^{-24}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481389 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ac193e9-56da-5e90-afd1-e67c5f8d1870" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480816} {:answer/id #uuid "602828b8-7fa4-51b0-ae3e-ca2c02d7c8af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{ln}^{2}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480917} {:answer/id #uuid "8be6f850-b0e1-5bd0-adfc-a35ba9264b99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481005} {:answer/id #uuid "b6110701-1956-577b-a189-4d9681a4e3ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{ln}^{2}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481086} {:answer/id #uuid "f0d1564e-78dd-55a4-b512-a4adf4fcad56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{ln}^{2}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481203}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0d1564e-78dd-55a4-b512-a4adf4fcad56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{ln}^{2}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481203} :answer-field/id #uuid "a913f95c-4f3f-51d9-a609-d167776b85b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481297}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7fe6b99e-c147-51fb-b13e-2b0491dcfd3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-83653" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y-2x)}{y-2x}\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y-2x\\le 4,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n1\\le y-2x\\le 4,\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=y-2x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 4,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y-2x)}{y-2x}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡u}{u}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡u}{u}\\,|-\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡u}{u}\\,du]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡u}{2}]}_{1}^{4}dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({ln}^{2}⁡4-{ln}^{2}⁡1)\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{ln}^{2}⁡4{\\int }_{0}^{2}dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{2}\\cdot [v]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & ={ln}^{2}⁡2\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =2{ln}^{2}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481407 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07e40d47-7a70-5f2c-ba24-6a855b1d6eb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{16}{3\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480757} {:answer/id #uuid "43bff162-e0e6-5e6e-b559-7547ee7c72a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480868} {:answer/id #uuid "e1b2263d-a0be-566e-8984-74cc5d983449" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{16}{5\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481164} {:answer/id #uuid "e83a9fc3-1293-5456-b3e0-ca1e41eb01ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481182} {:answer/id #uuid "e979451f-00ff-5ed9-83e8-7aff07a87414" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{32}{3\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481185}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e979451f-00ff-5ed9-83e8-7aff07a87414" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{32}{3\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481185} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e8c2182-159c-53aa-a9ec-6a0270a24944" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481282}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b0a81f04-6f59-512d-aa20-c54e3b26a51d" :question/math-academy-id "q-163277" :question/problem "![](images/06/06a853735916ae3219eea02fa7e7e30d9e941106600cd5b642d951f553aace59.png)\n\nFind the average value of the function $f(x,y)=5y({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})$ on the semicircle shown above." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\pi \\cdot {1}^{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe average value of the function over a region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at \n$(1,0).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that is mind, let's now write the $y=-\\sqrt{2x-{x}^{2}}$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\sqrt{2x-{x}^{2}} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =2x-{x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2x \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) & =2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2} & =2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTherefore, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 0\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}5y({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}5r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }5{r}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }5{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta [{r}^{5}]_{0}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ((2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{5}-0)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =32{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{5}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =32{[-\\frac{1}{6}{cos}^{6}⁡\\theta ]}_{-\\pi /2}^{0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{16}{3}[{cos}^{6}⁡(0)-{cos}^{6}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})] \\\\  & =-\\frac{16}{3}(1-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{16}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}\\cdot (-\\frac{16}{3})=-\\frac{32}{3\\pi }.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481408 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d66d8b4-c0c5-583c-9422-a56fec563fa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt[8]{e}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480858} {:answer/id #uuid "9d49fd2e-9748-5870-a942-7855eab45dc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(\\sqrt[4]{e}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481041} {:answer/id #uuid "aaec5f62-9f91-57f5-b958-6eca6bd1c1da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4(\\sqrt[4]{e}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481061} {:answer/id #uuid "c378d23d-c433-57b0-a302-3ca7d194c79d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4(\\sqrt[2]{e}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481117} {:answer/id #uuid "ff8cb142-efbd-5919-b474-a1189a94a948" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(\\sqrt[2]{e}+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481237}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aaec5f62-9f91-57f5-b958-6eca6bd1c1da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4(\\sqrt[4]{e}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481061} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1136c19-2541-5007-b027-75b59e3253ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481317}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b2ff4fa5-a334-50b5-9869-f8e569ec5011" :question/math-academy-id "q-163256" :question/problem "Find the average value of the function $f(x,y)={e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ on the disk of radius $\\frac{1}{2}$ centered at the origin." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\pi \\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nThe average value of the function over a region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le \\frac{1}{2},\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nUsing the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}{e}^{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{[\\frac{{e}^{{r}^{2}}}{2}]}_{0}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\frac{{e}^{1/4}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2})\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt[4]{e}-1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt[4]{e}-1}{2}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt[4]{e}-1}{2}(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =(\\sqrt[4]{e}-1)\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\cdot (\\sqrt[4]{e}-1)\\pi =4(\\sqrt[4]{e}-1).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481410 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11e03497-2478-5699-b8c8-60dc8393efd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480771} {:answer/id #uuid "14aeda53-bc0f-5285-a152-aac2c1de7b55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480775} {:answer/id #uuid "31768ff6-9f0c-50bd-9796-46c6ac637b1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480835} {:answer/id #uuid "328e9ba1-7166-5fcf-9c18-bf5334f7dc30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480837} {:answer/id #uuid "c05027eb-9d67-552f-950c-d7aa5524cbba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481107}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31768ff6-9f0c-50bd-9796-46c6ac637b1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480835} :answer-field/id #uuid "e051d3d7-1ee2-505b-957c-b010b42121b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481326}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b878a44e-123c-5f2f-a474-6dc1888b9c72" :question/math-academy-id "q-160938" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-xy)\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the quadrilateral region with vertices $(3,2),$ $(5,4),$ $(5,2),$ and $(3,0).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{2}-xy=x(x-y)\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v=x-y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(3,2) & =(3,3-2) \\\\  & =(3,1) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(5,4) & =(5,5-4) \\\\  & =(5,1) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(5,2) & =(5,5-2) \\\\  & =(5,3) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(3,0) & =(3,3-0) \\\\  & =(3,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,3\\le u\\le 5,\\,1\\le v\\le 3\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/e0/e0b810c03e7e3210fcff3a739e3781ad5ff694bfe44d5f73d7782c3b4108a286.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-1\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}x(x-y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}uv\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}uv\\,|-1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{3}^{5}uv\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}v\\,\\text{d}v{\\int }_{3}^{5}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}]}_{1}^{3}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{3}^{5} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481414 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93574e00-8aff-52c2-8fef-8d24d76bb02a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481016} {:answer/id #uuid "aee9d420-4960-50a5-922a-c43137c76d7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481067} {:answer/id #uuid "bc4a4294-c877-56f6-bfd0-3109ee79c13c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481097} {:answer/id #uuid "c00142a0-9805-5734-8860-3bf5256f853f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481104} {:answer/id #uuid "e631a5f0-8bc9-5fbb-b72c-fddfe67625ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481177}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e631a5f0-8bc9-5fbb-b72c-fddfe67625ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481177} :answer-field/id #uuid "3cad98c5-f0af-530e-9545-f63d666cd3ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481264}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c02d4f47-274a-5db8-9775-386bac66028f" :question/math-academy-id "q-102684" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{″}(1,2)$ for the function $f(x,y)=y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-{y}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-{y}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-{y}^{2})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{y}{x} & \\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2y\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{y}{x})=-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2y)=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{y}{x})=\\frac{1}{x}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2y)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate this matrix at $(1,2).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,2)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481418 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "062b1938-49ed-576d-84ed-09a8fb852cc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480749} {:answer/id #uuid "34aac4ef-6b3d-5a11-9211-3216b95b6351" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480841} {:answer/id #uuid "365aa022-a66e-5c42-b417-e96430223091" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{8}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480846} {:answer/id #uuid "9d25158d-398d-5c30-8fda-c6ddce1b0d76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{16}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481040} {:answer/id #uuid "f17962e7-50b9-5445-bd8a-61e21702bfc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481208}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f17962e7-50b9-5445-bd8a-61e21702bfc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481208} :answer-field/id #uuid "be68da14-ad39-5dd8-8b81-96f9bc5c3b1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481309}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c8466ab0-fd66-5adb-8220-fa89c46bae69" :question/math-academy-id "q-38309" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}y{cos}^{2}⁡(y-x)\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,|y-x|\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le y-x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=y-x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le u\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y{cos}^{2}⁡(y-x)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}v{cos}^{2}⁡u\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}v{cos}^{2}⁡u\\cdot |-1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}v[{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡u\\,du]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}v[{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2u+1)du]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}v[\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2u}{2}+u]_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\pi v\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}[\\frac{{v}^{2}}{2}]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot \\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481422 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a8cbc89-54fa-519f-83a7-e48a95410f62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480792} {:answer/id #uuid "426ae1b3-ab3f-577b-8b6d-7704b4df0833" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480866} {:answer/id #uuid "70a5ffa8-a36b-5443-9016-168b83a69d60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480948} {:answer/id #uuid "ad1ad46a-3ef4-5555-bffd-65ab07d26aab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481063} {:answer/id #uuid "b7359631-1d40-5953-b4f1-5c9499b96c8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481089}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a8cbc89-54fa-519f-83a7-e48a95410f62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480792} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc3a3607-0ff3-5794-9ec9-6e8573576500" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481308}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d1f6161d-c4cc-5098-b5d8-59c092ed97d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-39491" :question/problem "Given the orthogonal basis $B$ of ${R}^{3}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $3{x}_{2}$, where ${x}_{2}$ is the second component of $[x]_{B}.$\n\n$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2},\\,{q}_{3}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthogonal basis, we have that $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$, where ${x}_{i}=\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{i}}{{q}_{i}\\cdot {q}_{i}}.$ Computing ${x}_{2},$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{2}}{{q}_{2}\\cdot {q}_{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1+(-4)\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-8+2}{1+4+1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n3{x}_{2}=3\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{3})=-2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481423 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1761c3e4-c91a-57a8-9013-9b3654a234fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480783} {:answer/id #uuid "47c7196f-be5c-55f4-aa25-27610bf7736c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480875} {:answer/id #uuid "ae180a90-3ca2-5c61-ac30-22916f24dea1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481065} {:answer/id #uuid "e5aa3207-9a4b-5442-a65a-773ebcb88f61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481176} {:answer/id #uuid "f081f9d6-ce8e-5bb4-8583-a147cbb0c2be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481202}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47c7196f-be5c-55f4-aa25-27610bf7736c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480875} :answer-field/id #uuid "848b178b-00cd-5964-8724-b50b402d0b68" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481285}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d3e35845-58c1-5603-a038-b92edbbc2e02" :question/math-academy-id "q-160906" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+2y)\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the quadrilateral region with vertices $(1,-\\frac{1}{2}),$ $(\\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{3}{4}),$ $(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{\\pi -3}{4}),$ and $(1,\\frac{\\pi -2}{4}).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+2y)\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=2x,\\,v=x+2y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(1,-\\frac{1}{2}) & =(2\\cdot 1,1+2(-\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\  & =(2,0) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(\\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{3}{4}) & =(2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2},\\frac{3}{2}+2(-\\frac{3}{4})) \\\\  & =(3,0) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{\\pi -3}{4}) & =(2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2},\\frac{3}{2}+2(\\frac{\\pi -3}{4})) \\\\  & =(3,\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(1,\\frac{\\pi -2}{4}) & =(2\\cdot 1,1+2(\\frac{\\pi -2}{4})) \\\\  & =(2,\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 3,\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/dd/dd628fd99b4684d79e8c3b0f4e6696f72582abaef18eef729f5611711a2e42ec.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+2y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\,|\\frac{1}{4}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{2}^{3}u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\,\\text{d}v{\\int }_{2}^{3}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot [\\operatorname{sin}⁡v]_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{2}^{3}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 1\\cdot \\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481424 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "538437b0-2f11-5cf6-a513-3b5f95549b2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{(u-2v)}^{2}\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480889} {:answer/id #uuid "6ec46383-2d2f-5cde-b1c3-b222f3cbe2e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{(u-v)}^{2}\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480947} {:answer/id #uuid "7b7952ce-f2f3-57b0-b782-9aa012888b5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{(u-2v)}^{2}\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480968} {:answer/id #uuid "8271e591-b018-5489-b0eb-360dc3d744e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{(2u-v)}^{2}\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480988} {:answer/id #uuid "e3cd4b6c-e4fe-5b75-be7e-3479440b02e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{(u-v)}^{2}\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481170}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b7952ce-f2f3-57b0-b782-9aa012888b5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{(u-2v)}^{2}\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480968} :answer-field/id #uuid "912cb459-ddba-5110-ba83-e5c7cb9c0fab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481289}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d52198ba-3323-5776-a083-de931434cfe0" :question/math-academy-id "q-82798" :question/problem "Consider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le 2y-x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y-x\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nBy performing the change of variables $u=2y-x$ and $v=y-x,$ the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\,dx\\,dy\n$$\n\ncan be written in terms of $u$ and $v$ as" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=2y-x,\\,v=y-x\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nIn this case, we first need to solve the transformation equations\n\n$$\nu=2y-x,\\,v=y-x\n$$\n\n \nfor $x$ and $y$, which gives\n\n$$\nx=u-2v,\\,y=u-v.\n$$\n\n \n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}{(u-2v)}^{2}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{(u-2v)}^{2}\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{(u-2v)}^{2}\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481429 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fed0bd2-6515-5d71-9deb-11c96d0f5a24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({u}^{2}+u-v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480830} {:answer/id #uuid "50d5f25b-3bff-52d9-b4d2-40d73df06c36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({u}^{2}-v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480888} {:answer/id #uuid "632142b3-3dde-53ef-9fc3-71eb83cdcd38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({u}^{2}-v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480921} {:answer/id #uuid "844f29d7-a7a5-5823-a2f4-5f8ba38566ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({u}^{2}+v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480993} {:answer/id #uuid "b71f8dcb-5c9d-54b5-b09b-deeeea423e1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({u}^{2}+u-v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481088}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2fed0bd2-6515-5d71-9deb-11c96d0f5a24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({u}^{2}+u-v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480830} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ab7f9c1-3d45-58a6-bbb6-a63febcef757" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481286}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e0897fe5-68e3-5373-b337-c33b7a65cd54" :question/math-academy-id "q-82818" :question/problem "Consider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 0,\\,\\,1-(x+1)^{2}\\le y\\le 2-(x+1)^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nBy performing the change of variables $u=x+1$ and $v=y+(x+1)^{2},$ the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(x-y+1)\\,dx\\,dy\n$$\n\ncan be written in terms of $u$ and $v$ as" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+1,\\,v=y+(x+1)^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows::\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,1\\le v\\le 2\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2(x+1) & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nIn this case, we first need to solve the transformation equations\n\n$$\nu=x+1,\\,v=y+(x+1)^{2}\n$$\n\n \nfor $x$ and $y$, which gives\n\n$$\nx=u-1,\\,y=v-{u}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(x-y+1)\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}[(u-1)-(v-{u}^{2})+1]|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}({u}^{2}+u-v)\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}({u}^{2}+u-v)\\cdot |1|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({u}^{2}+u-v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481430 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73ea0adf-89e3-52c6-a0cd-0e6c3a1d6e20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480952} {:answer/id #uuid "7e36322d-c7bd-5d73-a142-eb2532e6eb77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480976} {:answer/id #uuid "db5ef865-a8de-5c3e-835e-5fc660d5d30b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{105}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481153} {:answer/id #uuid "f07c8233-f2b2-5d87-8d57-e59421760285" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481201} {:answer/id #uuid "f372503b-18dc-5dbe-8608-da48adbf20c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{35}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481211}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db5ef865-a8de-5c3e-835e-5fc660d5d30b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{105}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481153} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f7c61ba-8de7-54e4-9c9a-0d1e7cbb1333" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481251}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e4b188aa-dc23-5bc9-90f6-b5a36b6d8b4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-160902" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(2{x}^{2}-xy-{y}^{2})\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the quadrilateral region with vertices $(1,-1),$ $(2,0),$ $(3,-2),$ and $(2,-3).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2{x}^{2}-xy-{y}^{2}=(2x+y)(x-y)\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=2x+y,\\,v=x-y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(1,-1) & =(2(1)+(-1),1-(-1)) \\\\  & =(1,2) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(2,0) & =(2(2)+0,2-0) \\\\  & =(4,2) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(3,-2) & =(2(3)+(-2),3-(-2)) \\\\  & =(4,5) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(2,-3) & =(2(2)+(-3),2-(-3)) \\\\  & =(1,5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 4,\\,2\\le v\\le 5\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/9f/9f5030bc3437fbc4431dd5350620bc7f9133d2bf910654607c0c5f7ef292f3a3.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(2{x}^{2}-xy-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}(2x+y)(x-y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}uv\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}uv\\,|-\\frac{1}{3}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{2}^{5}{\\int }_{1}^{4}uv\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{2}^{5}v\\,\\text{d}v{\\int }_{1}^{4}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}]}_{2}^{5}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{21}{2}\\cdot \\frac{15}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{105}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481431 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6364af2a-3d6b-5851-9049-3595e8cfce71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480922} {:answer/id #uuid "8236c364-9a58-5bd5-bca8-e007796586fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480987} {:answer/id #uuid "91e046c8-5b65-5972-9eef-cd8f897a8cd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481012} {:answer/id #uuid "dbc70cb2-b436-5aca-b9d7-fabe9b0e5de6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481154} {:answer/id #uuid "fc8d0347-5303-50ad-8ea6-72f1ba59d8dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481229}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc8d0347-5303-50ad-8ea6-72f1ba59d8dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481229} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b345d81-65d6-5a3f-9150-c97ad1b7b016" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481275}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ea750ce2-b7b3-5ba2-80fa-7d5a87470671" :question/math-academy-id "q-160850" :question/problem "Using the change of variables $u=x+y$ and $v=x-y,$ evaluate the double integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(x+y)(x-y)^{3/2}\\,dx\\,dy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x+y\\le 3,\\,0\\le x-y\\le 1\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=x-y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }-1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(x+y)(x-y)^{3/2}\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}u{v}^{3/2}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{3}u{v}^{3/2}|-\\frac{1}{2}|du\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{v}^{3/2}[{\\int }_{1}^{3}u\\,du]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{v}^{3/2}{[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{3}\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{v}^{3/2}[\\frac{9}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}]dv \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{v}^{3/2}\\,dv \\\\  & =2{[\\frac{2}{5}\\,{v}^{5/2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}[1-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481432 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71a05bae-9b38-579f-bf4d-77a5a3ed21ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13({e}^{2}-1)}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480950} {:answer/id #uuid "78a5344d-2607-521a-bc5c-b422c76ae208" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{e}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480960} {:answer/id #uuid "c8d2beaf-f051-5e9a-a9bb-d9e392012d19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4e-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481120} {:answer/id #uuid "e262d3c6-319b-5ade-8d21-fe6baf79d69d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4(e-1)}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481168} {:answer/id #uuid "ff742366-fc6b-51c9-9bb1-c3bf559e492f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481236}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71a05bae-9b38-579f-bf4d-77a5a3ed21ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13({e}^{2}-1)}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480950} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e6427b8-37d4-5988-9502-b3fd4454d9b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481272}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ebe24ae4-1aba-5953-99d8-8533d35f69cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-160918" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+2xy+{y}^{2}){e}^{x-y}\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x+y\\le 3,\\,0\\le x-y\\le 2\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n1\\le x+y\\le 3,\\,0\\le x-y\\le 2,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x+y\\,\\text{and}\\,v=x-y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }\\text{ }1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+2xy+{y}^{2}){e}^{x-y}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}(x+y)^{2}{e}^{x-y}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{u}^{2}{e}^{v}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{u}^{2}{e}^{v}|-\\frac{1}{2}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{u}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{v}\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{u}^{2}[{e}^{v}]_{0}^{2}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{u}^{2}\\cdot ({e}^{2}-1)\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{2}{[\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3}]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{26}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{13({e}^{2}-1)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481434 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1db0d9f5-1143-5b9b-b945-fa7c0b7aaf13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480801} {:answer/id #uuid "5d785d71-3068-5db5-be3e-a22f94c4720c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480912} {:answer/id #uuid "67c466f7-5e58-5990-9bfa-36a6b7e3de81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480930} {:answer/id #uuid "bd2f30f4-4c96-59d7-80a8-398429f8a06c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481101} {:answer/id #uuid "dc8d324b-b300-53c5-91d4-de7ddf595c6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481157}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67c466f7-5e58-5990-9bfa-36a6b7e3de81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480930} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba1ace49-0ce8-5f3b-a500-296b7a164c42" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481306}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f47eca8f-5f35-574c-b95e-5dc9a27cb04d" :question/math-academy-id "q-160916" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y-x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le x+y\\le \\pi \\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n0\\le y-x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le x+y\\le \\pi ,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=y-x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=x+y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le v\\le \\pi \\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ \\text{ }\\text{ }\\text{ }1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v|-\\frac{1}{2}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot [-\\operatorname{cos}⁡v]_{0}^{\\pi }\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot 2\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,du \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{sin}⁡u]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481440 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e55ce08-51f3-5f7d-9882-1ad73c59bda5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480765} {:answer/id #uuid "8273dafa-bda1-5a66-9cff-9b7575876a40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480989} {:answer/id #uuid "bcd0ae49-abbe-5447-a383-493cedc60a77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481100} {:answer/id #uuid "e59d2e21-5875-56c6-8566-91c968ba9e4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481175} {:answer/id #uuid "ec2a5b9c-1081-52b7-b89e-e7f3524f0dee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481194}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bcd0ae49-abbe-5447-a383-493cedc60a77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481100} :answer-field/id #uuid "7505a4fd-3c5e-5a38-a1f7-3891a3c2965c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481279}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fee52d9d-67c4-5104-9f57-4122f892110d" :question/math-academy-id "q-82911" :question/problem "Using the change of variables $u=x+y$ and $v=x-y,$ evaluate the double integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,dx\\,dy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x+y\\le 2,\\,2\\le x-y\\le 4\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=x-y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,2\\le v\\le 4\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }-1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}(x+y)(x-y)|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}uv\\cdot |-\\frac{1}{2}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{∬}_{Δ}uv\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}u[{\\int }_{2}^{4}v\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}u{[\\frac{{v}^{2}}{2}]}_{2}^{4}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}u(\\frac{{4}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2})\\,du \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2}u\\,du \\\\  & =3[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481560 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a577eb1-695c-55d3-9958-742bb165e9fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 x + 3 y=28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481549} {:answer/id #uuid "590f4196-5afc-597c-8846-19186099e725" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 x + 3 y=22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481552} {:answer/id #uuid "5e450849-303a-5568-9866-2d4dcc0ac383" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 x -3 y=-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481555} {:answer/id #uuid "c74de8a8-dc89-584b-8e91-b63fb64475a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 x -3 y=-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481559} {:answer/id #uuid "f51e9b94-ae4a-51bf-8264-6a0f64a4fde9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x + y=11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481562}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a577eb1-695c-55d3-9958-742bb165e9fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 x + 3 y=28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481549} :answer-field/id #uuid "a04717ff-2885-50c6-ab11-6aff98029a55" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481557}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c7aa6290-5c44-5145-bf1e-dc13974023ae" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(5,6)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(1,5)$ and $(-3,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "Perpendicular lines have slopes which are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(1,5)$ and $(-3,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m_1 &= \\frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1} \\\\ &= \\frac{-1-(5)}{-3-(1)} \\\\ &= \\frac{-6}{-4} \\\\ &= \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of the new line should be the negative reciprocal of the slope of the given line. The given line has a slope of $\\frac{3}{2},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=-\\frac{2}{3}$ and pass through the point $(x_1,y_1)=(5,6).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-y_1 &= m(x-x_1) \\\\ y-(6) &= -\\frac{2}{3}(x-(5)) \\\\ y-(6) &= \\frac{10}{3} -\\frac{2 x}{3} \\\\ y &= \\frac{28}{3} -\\frac{2 x}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we want to write the equation in standard form. So, we will bring the $x$ term to the left-hand side and multiply both sides of the equation by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= \\frac{28}{3} -\\frac{2 x}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2 x}{3} + y &= \\frac{28}{3} \\\\ 3(\\frac{2 x}{3} + y) &= 3(\\frac{28}{3}) \\\\ 2 x + 3 y &= 28\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481663 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17341934-1f7c-517d-8c9b-00c031b8823a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{r}^{2}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481590} {:answer/id #uuid "40b07501-069c-515d-b0e4-25b6aea2080b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{|r|}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481600} {:answer/id #uuid "684728cb-9a8a-5e94-9dd1-04eb633a68d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm \\frac{r}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481606} {:answer/id #uuid "7ab85dd4-a67f-5d96-9874-b5311a0fcf49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{|r|}{100}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481611} {:answer/id #uuid "e281c12c-09da-5127-85cb-7d8cc84bf743" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{r}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481638}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40b07501-069c-515d-b0e4-25b6aea2080b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{|r|}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481600} :answer-field/id #uuid "903290b8-9d04-50ab-9ab9-3b30be15c773" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481658}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1ea1bc4b-df65-5c2e-8a1e-25b530d6cd97" :question/math-academy-id "q-129465" :question/problem "$\\sqrt{\\frac{{r}^{2}}{100}}=$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the square root of a perfect square can be simplified using absolute value.\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{x}^{2}}=|x|\n$$\n\nAlso, we notice that ${r}^{2}$ and $100$ are both perfect squares.\n\nFirst, we write the expression under the square root as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{{r}^{2}}{100}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{r}^{2}}{{10}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{r\\cdot r}{10\\cdot 10}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{r}{10}\\cdot \\frac{r}{10}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{r}{10})}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we take the absolute value of the squared quantity and simplify using the rules of absolute value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{r}{10})}^{2}} & =|\\frac{r}{10}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|r|}{|10|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|r|}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481667 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c04b137-6afd-5c93-bffd-c2ad52b0f5a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{85.25}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481581} {:answer/id #uuid "14660518-3268-5b10-8494-338fa52ec7ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{58.52}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481587} {:answer/id #uuid "33a073ec-2091-5bcd-b60b-ebba7bccb98d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{25.58}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481595} {:answer/id #uuid "4cd33462-6550-5cd3-834f-318520fe8bc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{55.82}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481601} {:answer/id #uuid "c8e3caac-58c5-5c9f-b488-f943b767b28f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{52.58}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481632}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14660518-3268-5b10-8494-338fa52ec7ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{58.52}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481587} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc9aaa6a-a4ef-548d-866f-aa031c7198d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481661}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "887e0ea0-dd4d-5fcd-a5bc-286204a862b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-40217" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the acute angle between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal.   Round the answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 0}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{a}_{1}=(1)\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-2)+3\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x$ or any other non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ So, to make calculations easier, we can find the angle between the vectors\n\n$\\,$ $x=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,3\\,{\\text{proj}}_{S}x=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 5+1\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}}\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{11}\\sqrt{27}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}\\sqrt{11}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{33}}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{33}}{11})≈{58.52}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482511 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8685f08f-d476-5822-b98a-1d7e181d364e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482089} {:answer/id #uuid "abcdf334-5232-5108-815d-8544de96b5b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482177} {:answer/id #uuid "acad01e2-2ec4-510b-8062-e56c2b02423d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482180} {:answer/id #uuid "b73ed2ab-45c7-58bf-8285-14105b905660" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482210} {:answer/id #uuid "ee553bba-00ce-56b8-8960-df4b7c0e9646" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482343}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee553bba-00ce-56b8-8960-df4b7c0e9646" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482343} :answer-field/id #uuid "e977d9ba-fc00-5472-b57e-d5737f833b3e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482496}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "15e16006-8a30-5d22-b639-4bcd0e964462" :question/math-academy-id "q-39058" :question/problem "Consider the finite region $R$ in the $xyz$-space, given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,\\pi \\le x+y\\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le y+z\\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le z\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nGiven the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=y+z,\\,w=z,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the missing part of the expression below?\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y+z)\\,dV={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\bbox[3pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAA}}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$ \n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=y+z,\\,w=z,\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps: \n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,\\pi \\le u\\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le v\\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le w\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y+z)\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\cdot |\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\cdot |1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\,\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482515 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "524eda41-ddb8-5f48-ab3e-43c84e18bcd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{-uv}^{3}u(w+v)\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481972} {:answer/id #uuid "8c235bdb-c968-5d76-8236-6fd1197822e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{u-v}^{3}\\frac{v(w+v)}{2u}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482102} {:answer/id #uuid "cbe036c6-1bc2-5a04-8662-ddfdd4bc5170" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{u+v}^{2}u(w+v)\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482258} {:answer/id #uuid "e72adb6e-7faa-57b5-8126-d2e42feeb89a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{-v}^{3}\\frac{v(w+v)}{2u}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482327} {:answer/id #uuid "f97b8fcd-b3a2-520e-9d92-adc67193b681" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{-v}^{3}\\frac{u(w+v)}{2v}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482364}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e72adb6e-7faa-57b5-8126-d2e42feeb89a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{-v}^{3}\\frac{v(w+v)}{2u}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482327} :answer-field/id #uuid "d68eca02-5016-5081-b3c3-150bae83f3f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482491}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "22a02818-7cda-52f6-bd03-f172a29927f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-161892" :question/problem "Consider the finite region $R$ in the $xyz$-space, given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le \\frac{y}{x}\\le 2,\\,1\\le xy\\le 3,\\,0\\le z\\le xy+3\\}.\n$$\n\nBy performing the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{y}{x},\\,v=xy,\\,w=z-xy,\n$$\n\nthe triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}xyz\\,dV\n$$\n\ncan be written in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w$ as" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{y}{x},\\,v=xy,\\,w=z-xy,\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,1\\le v\\le 3,\\,-v\\le w\\le 3\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x} & 0 \\\\ y & x & 0 \\\\ -y & -x & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ y & x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{y}{x}-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2y}{x} \\\\  & =-2u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}xyz\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}v(w+v)\\cdot |-\\frac{1}{2u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{v(w+v)}{2u}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{-v}^{3}\\frac{v(w+v)}{2u}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482517 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c4d79bc-915f-5faf-978f-4d42e48a1002" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481886} {:answer/id #uuid "55c24ac5-9fd4-503b-ba36-10fa741c0864" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481978} {:answer/id #uuid "78eefeb0-a4ae-5e62-bcd9-4a983105c3d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482054} {:answer/id #uuid "793af677-077b-5623-8d55-c294d9651a32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482055} {:answer/id #uuid "e345bb2f-b85f-50d4-b376-0cd925292cbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482313}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "793af677-077b-5623-8d55-c294d9651a32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482055} :answer-field/id #uuid "9860bd94-686f-562b-93e2-7506456e5e9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482458}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2b515338-9a49-5088-8d83-3ec16988abce" :question/math-academy-id "q-94641" :question/problem "Find the median of the random variable $Y$ if the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}6{e}^{-6y},\\, & y\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The median of a random variable $Y$ is the number $m$ such that\n\n$$\nP(Y\\le m)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\le m) & ={\\int }_{0}^{m}6{e}^{-6y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{e}^{-6y}{|}_{0}^{m} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-6m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the median is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-{e}^{-6m} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {e}^{-6m} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -6m & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ -6m & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ m & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482523 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25237418-8897-5984-a318-b9d6acfd3dbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(p-\\frac{9}{16})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481872} {:answer/id #uuid "336a6d96-d960-535c-95fb-75d4c09869fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(p-\\frac{9}{4})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481900} {:answer/id #uuid "4e12cabb-23ac-5417-9a7a-ef3c27b9cbcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(p-\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481965} {:answer/id #uuid "6481951b-42c4-5e4f-90f7-2774c2f713bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(p-\\frac{3}{16})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482006} {:answer/id #uuid "8350d09e-a155-59ee-9cf8-3c2e7a019dc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(p-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482076}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e12cabb-23ac-5417-9a7a-ef3c27b9cbcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(p-\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481965} :answer-field/id #uuid "68e62d53-fe58-5a69-aba9-9a9c52260323" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482430}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "33ad6f1c-10b4-54e8-b066-192c2a7ae636" :question/math-academy-id "q-111620" :question/problem "Factor ${p}^{2}-\\frac{3}{2}p+\\frac{9}{16}.$" :question/worked-solution "The \"square of a difference\" formula states that\n\n$$\n{p}^{2}-2ap+{a}^{2}=(p-a)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\n{p}^{2}-\\frac{3}{2}p+\\frac{9}{16}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the constant term as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{2}-\\frac{3}{2}p+{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}p+{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of difference formula with $a=\\frac{3}{4}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(p-\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482529 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12f1b3aa-ebc1-51c1-8273-c4b915d8ecf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(n+\\frac{25}{16})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481826} {:answer/id #uuid "3d534e72-0930-51b5-a1d0-f3aa1de73e46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(n+\\frac{5}{4})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481928} {:answer/id #uuid "7e777da0-d7e0-58cc-ba96-49a1a74e9598" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(n+\\frac{5}{16})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482064} {:answer/id #uuid "898c7c3f-dd8f-5773-abc2-e29cff260011" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(n+\\frac{25}{4})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482094} {:answer/id #uuid "9d033726-a4f4-50d9-b131-7a5467c7dc90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(n+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482148}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d534e72-0930-51b5-a1d0-f3aa1de73e46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(n+\\frac{5}{4})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481928} :answer-field/id #uuid "6650d077-6bfe-5e18-8868-b906e55313a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482428}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3ab2e3ce-42ef-5550-a256-e8688e7ed48b" :question/math-academy-id "q-111618" :question/problem "Factor the expression ${n}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}n+\\frac{25}{16}.$" :question/worked-solution "The \"square of a sum\" formula states that\n\n$$\n{n}^{2}+2an+{a}^{2}=(n+a)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\n{n}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}n+\\frac{25}{16}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the constant term as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}n+{(\\frac{5}{4})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{5}{4}n+{(\\frac{5}{4})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of sum formula with $a=\\frac{5}{4}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(n+\\frac{5}{4})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482530 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1536ecd2-34e1-5711-8194-9090d29369f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481834} {:answer/id #uuid "857fe175-09f7-5eb2-89ec-46c4061ca387" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482087} {:answer/id #uuid "ab7b47f3-3802-5868-b5d4-8e9d5d18bbcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482176} {:answer/id #uuid "b3a944d1-2a3f-544a-a5da-c1c851119eb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482198} {:answer/id #uuid "e454e28a-b46a-5bb3-81f3-e70638e4c982" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482316}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab7b47f3-3802-5868-b5d4-8e9d5d18bbcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482176} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5adb4bd-431e-53be-8fd6-9773762a903f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482489}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3b7c4644-c6ca-51e8-b34c-e72d6ef6b667" :question/math-academy-id "q-161894" :question/problem "Use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=y+z,\\,w=z,\n$$\n\nto evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y+z)\\,dV,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,\\pi \\le x+y\\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le y+z\\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le z\\le 1\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=y+z,\\,w=z,\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,\\pi \\le u\\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le v\\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le w\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y+z)\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\cdot |-1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\,dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}dw \\\\  & =[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u]_{u=\\pi }^{u=2\\pi }\\cdot {[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡v]}_{v=0}^{v=\\pi }\\cdot [w]_{w=0}^{w=1} \\\\  & =(-1+(-1))\\cdot (-(-1)+1)\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482534 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a545db6-b2c1-5af7-bab8-a92a067e183d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3w}\\frac{1}{3}(u+\\frac{vw}{u})\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481792} {:answer/id #uuid "68e6ba86-dd63-5072-80c7-8937c30e291d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{v}(\\frac{w}{u}+3vu)\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482015} {:answer/id #uuid "6c9f7ec3-cf1f-5f6e-8b5d-a4981ae3628c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3w}\\frac{1}{3}(v+\\frac{vw}{u})\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482026} {:answer/id #uuid "ad6f11e6-c760-5ae7-8acd-14a00b864038" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}(\\frac{u}{3}+\\frac{vw}{u})\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482181} {:answer/id #uuid "e4dfc26b-d49c-54ee-9547-1ec07e222142" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{3}(v+\\frac{vw}{u})\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482319}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4dfc26b-d49c-54ee-9547-1ec07e222142" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{3}(v+\\frac{vw}{u})\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482319} :answer-field/id #uuid "176c3a3c-6656-5311-869f-fdb6f745be21" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482389}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "46b1b539-1890-56ee-b628-8f298fa77da5" :question/math-academy-id "q-161871" :question/problem "Consider the finite region $R$ in the $xyz$-space, given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le xy\\le 2,\\,0\\le z\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nBy performing the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v=xy,\\,w=3z,\n$$\n\n \n\nthe triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}({x}^{2}y+3xyz)\\,dV\n$$\n\ncan be written in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w$ as" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v=xy,\\,w=3z,\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 2,\\,0\\le w\\le 3\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ y & x & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ y & x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3(x-0) \\\\  & =3x \\\\  & =3u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{3u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}({x}^{2}y+3xyz)\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}(uv+vw)\\cdot |\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,du\\,dv\\,dw \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}(uv+vw)\\cdot |\\frac{1}{3u}|\\,du\\,dv\\,dw \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\,\\frac{1}{3}(v+\\frac{vw}{u})\\,\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482540 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0eca66db-cc53-5a4d-aca7-eaf9a7ab3b31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{2}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{2}^{2w}w\\operatorname{ln}⁡u\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481811} {:answer/id #uuid "47bf8ad5-d857-5c65-9120-529840b5d37f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{2}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{3}^{6}\\frac{w\\operatorname{ln}⁡u}{3u}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481950} {:answer/id #uuid "d8a29ad8-5f0f-54a0-a2fe-7f5f6a9c57e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{2}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{3}^{w}\\frac{w\\operatorname{ln}⁡v}{2v}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482289} {:answer/id #uuid "f069b42f-d4b1-545c-b713-4a2714ba1128" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{2}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{1}^{3w}\\frac{w\\operatorname{ln}⁡u}{6}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482347} {:answer/id #uuid "fa08f136-85ba-53cf-9533-f666c9d80525" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{2}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{1}^{2w}\\frac{w\\operatorname{ln}⁡u}{6u}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482367}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47bf8ad5-d857-5c65-9120-529840b5d37f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{2}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{3}^{6}\\frac{w\\operatorname{ln}⁡u}{3u}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481950} :answer-field/id #uuid "976640a1-1292-535e-95ff-f6793512061b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482457}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4f86e12d-91a6-559a-805d-f5f0798ca229" :question/math-academy-id "q-161889" :question/problem "Consider the finite region $R$ in the $xyz$-space, given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,2\\le x-y\\le 3,\\,1\\le {x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\le 4,\\,1\\le z\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\nBy performing the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x-y,\\,v={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\,w=3z,\n$$\n\nthe triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}6z\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)\\,dV\n$$\n\ncan be written in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w$ as" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x-y,\\,v={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\,w=3z,\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 3,\\,1\\le v\\le 4,\\,3\\le w\\le 6\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2x & -2y & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2x & -2y\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3(-2y+2x) \\\\  & =6(x-y) \\\\  & =6u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{6u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}6z\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}2w\\operatorname{ln}⁡u\\cdot |-\\frac{1}{6u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{w\\operatorname{ln}⁡u}{3u}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{3}^{6}\\frac{w\\operatorname{ln}⁡u}{3u}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482547 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b43c30d-dbbd-56ae-bb9d-ec705a2a3b5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481795} {:answer/id #uuid "0d41d4b8-c95d-5007-abd0-08600085f08f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481803} {:answer/id #uuid "11f2f0e2-ee85-5dc7-8bf8-e4bbb3c1a106" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481822} {:answer/id #uuid "e6ad71c3-d535-5d86-9412-6ac3d6c65116" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482325} {:answer/id #uuid "f3d483b0-65b2-53a0-9add-ad98d8a18577" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482353}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d41d4b8-c95d-5007-abd0-08600085f08f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481803} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6f66c5c-6087-5c29-8319-6c31eb512359" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482500}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7144ca6a-debb-5e59-baf7-8232b8798b6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-162183" :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 3,\\,-2\\le x-z\\le 0,\\,1\\le \\frac{z-1}{y}\\le 5\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n1\\le y\\le 3,\\,-2\\le x-z\\le 0,\\,1\\le \\frac{z-1}{y}\\le 5,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=y,\\,v=x-z,\\,w=\\frac{z-1}{y}.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,-2\\le v\\le 0,\\,1\\le w\\le 5\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{z-1}{{y}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-1)^{1+2}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=u.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |u|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{1}^{5}u\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}u\\,du\\cdot {\\int }_{-2}^{0}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{5}dw \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}]}_{u=1}^{u=3}\\cdot [v]_{v=-2}^{v=0}\\cdot [w]_{w=1}^{w=5} \\\\  & =(\\frac{9}{2}-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (0-(-2))\\cdot (5-1) \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482578 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04417ca8-5b44-5f47-8588-203cc9670c83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{p}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{3}{4}p+\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481779} {:answer/id #uuid "3b77de52-656a-5d60-a77d-89b9ba362ce9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{p}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481921} {:answer/id #uuid "53a9e444-9bb4-5607-8e5c-dff301ba180a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{p}^{2}}{2}+3p+\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481976} {:answer/id #uuid "c668be38-d1ab-5b18-93b6-f9c30310d732" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{p}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482244} {:answer/id #uuid "f6ec47ef-9394-5b6f-bf61-3e7cfa86d762" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{p}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{3}{2}p+\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482360}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6ec47ef-9394-5b6f-bf61-3e7cfa86d762" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{p}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{3}{2}p+\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482360} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd6480f1-db17-5206-8b50-adeb65376df5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482479}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ae2cfcdb-b465-5141-b188-02dc5998e9f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-33092" :question/problem "${(\\frac{p}{2}+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the following formula:\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{2}={a}^{2}+2ab+{b}^{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=\\frac{p}{2}$ and $b=\\frac{3}{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{p}{2}+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} &= {(\\frac{p}{2})}^{2}+2(\\frac{p}{2})(\\frac{3}{2})+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} \\\\ &= \\frac{{p}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{3}{2}p+\\frac{9}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482580 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c637b6f-4f06-51ca-9616-b193dbfe0073" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481798} {:answer/id #uuid "1bb8c5ce-8dbf-5386-8235-8248286f77f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481846} {:answer/id #uuid "4ab67b62-04e2-59bc-825a-dfe5cf1d691d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481954} {:answer/id #uuid "547236d6-bb1e-5d75-97ea-1f9bb0063831" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481977} {:answer/id #uuid "dc7f0181-a616-56cf-9ee3-cd568924243c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482297}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc7f0181-a616-56cf-9ee3-cd568924243c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482297} :answer-field/id #uuid "694f0a99-bbd9-58dc-bc56-6c2fef66f7a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482432}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b4b2886a-abaf-53c5-8dbb-583291c4a37e" :question/math-academy-id "q-162211" :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,0\\le z-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1)\\le 2\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,0\\le z-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1)\\le 2,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v=y,\\,w=z-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1).\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 2,\\,0\\le w\\le 2\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{2y}{{y}^{2}+1} & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}dw \\\\  & =[u]_{u=0}^{u=2}\\cdot [v]_{v=0}^{v=2}\\cdot [w]_{w=0}^{w=2} \\\\  & =(2-0)\\cdot (2-0)\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482599 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13f0ef2e-0f93-5e0a-8d71-92286357a7c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481828} {:answer/id #uuid "2836c933-6423-5d47-b0d5-a225bde1662d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481878} {:answer/id #uuid "d2729257-14f7-5d82-bd92-97d5a5b741d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482278} {:answer/id #uuid "da0e5662-6a8a-576b-b15e-de600a9ace05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482294} {:answer/id #uuid "fe01dfce-1c89-5728-a40a-4b936e3d1215" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482376}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2836c933-6423-5d47-b0d5-a225bde1662d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481878} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0305ffe-7471-5805-bce3-ea016f3e92ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482486}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d8c9e8ae-5eea-50e8-acfe-85f90d5a9922" :question/math-academy-id "q-161933" :question/problem "Use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=y+z,\\,v=x+4z,\\,w=2y\n$$\n\nto evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\,\\frac{y+z}{x+4z}\\,dV,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y+z\\le 2,\\,1\\le x+4z\\le e,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=y+z,\\,v=x+4z,\\,w=2y\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,1\\le v\\le e,\\,0\\le w\\le 2\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(-1)^{3+2}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(-1)(-1). \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{y+z}{x+4z}\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}\\cdot |\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{2v}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{u}{2v}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{u}{2}\\,du\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{v}\\,dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}dw \\\\  & =[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{4}]_{u=0}^{u=2}\\cdot {[\\operatorname{ln}⁡v]}_{v=1}^{v=e}\\cdot [w]_{w=0}^{w=2} \\\\  & =(1-0)\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482601 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "107bc90b-168a-5ae7-9c1e-5151de594035" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481814} {:answer/id #uuid "1508999f-583d-5409-a945-a2774f7b6030" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481833} {:answer/id #uuid "1e78963c-6d08-52e4-886b-c299ac1eab3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481855} {:answer/id #uuid "a5dfe954-7d04-5a12-8506-1f9267e2a525" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482169} {:answer/id #uuid "fdb401e4-0e59-57f0-b798-ca38f29d946f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482375}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1508999f-583d-5409-a945-a2774f7b6030" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481833} :answer-field/id #uuid "748331ec-5745-5dc8-9854-65c69f6cb19e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482439}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ddc59e97-e243-5e27-9d57-5e0f9b714882" :question/math-academy-id "q-161777" :question/problem "![](images/90/90d5e9115d216f2a7f8fba49295a36b95cd932c4df7f8bace1b45e3542e9ae11.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})}^{3}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ for $x\\ge 0$ and $z\\ge 0,$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents one-quarter of the sphere of radius $1$ centered at the origin.\n\n![](images/ec/ec2f6c67eee7f836cc71efec2b17c2276aa8a9d397c632b3c499a46a75f7b95c.png)\n\n \n\nAs a result, we obtain that in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 1,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)={({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})}^{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & ={({\\rho }^{2})}^{3} \\\\  & ={\\rho }^{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})}^{3}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}{\\rho }^{6}\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\rho }^{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,dϕ\\cdot {\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}d\\theta \\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\rho }^{8}\\,d\\rho  \\\\  & =[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ]_{ϕ=0}^{ϕ=\\pi /2}\\cdot [\\theta ]_{\\theta =-\\pi /2}^{\\theta =\\pi /2}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{9}{\\rho }^{9}]}_{\\rho =0}^{\\rho =1} \\\\  & =-(0-1)\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}))\\cdot \\frac{1}{9}(1-0) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482606 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ee0c1d0-b902-5d6e-9fd8-7baec2f61f9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481859} {:answer/id #uuid "572c02e1-9d71-5b99-960a-6f12882be763" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481981} {:answer/id #uuid "70ef93b1-e501-54d3-bcff-1f06883cd750" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482035} {:answer/id #uuid "75475609-dfeb-560d-bc22-9379064434fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482047} {:answer/id #uuid "8e5aafc8-ceba-5678-a412-98f1f904e581" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482109}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ee0c1d0-b902-5d6e-9fd8-7baec2f61f9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481859} :answer-field/id #uuid "3587c6f6-e4fb-567f-a4f3-80de70f97f45" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482407}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e662cc33-8ce5-5a82-9731-097b3595e6c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-162140" :question/problem "Use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x+{y}^{2},\\,v=y+z,\\,w=z\n$$\n\nto evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}(x+{y}^{2})^{9}z\\,dV,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x+{y}^{2}\\le 1,\\,0\\le y+z\\le 5,\\,0\\le z\\le 4\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+{y}^{2},\\,v=y+z,\\,w=z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 5,\\,0\\le w\\le 2\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2y & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}(x+{y}^{2})^{9}z\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}{u}^{9}w\\cdot |1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}{u}^{9}w\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{5}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{u}^{9}w\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{9}\\,du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{5}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{4}w\\,dw \\\\  & =[\\frac{{u}^{10}}{10}]_{u=0}^{u=1}\\cdot {[v]}_{v=0}^{v=5}\\cdot [\\frac{{w}^{2}}{2}]_{w=0}^{w=4} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{10}-0)\\cdot (5-0)\\cdot (8-0) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482615 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42bc746b-b715-55f2-86ec-fd72bb4f4ef2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481937} {:answer/id #uuid "4c8522ed-fdef-5b29-b95a-c55a7f972e48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481957} {:answer/id #uuid "d1f28ea2-1428-5d1b-859c-d4b26aa341ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482276} {:answer/id #uuid "dd5075d2-9ee4-5c53-a21e-1de9889cd0a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482299} {:answer/id #uuid "fa7b48d0-b052-528a-ae87-b2b5f4f33257" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482369}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1f28ea2-1428-5d1b-859c-d4b26aa341ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482276} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d2256b3-469f-5f0e-a313-ab48af9bfa87" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482393}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f69775da-3c2e-512a-8912-f342f7d0a00f" :question/math-academy-id "q-162153" :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y+3z\\le 2,\\,1\\le {z}^{3}\\le 8\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y+3z\\le 2,\\,1\\le {z}^{3}\\le 8,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v=y+3z,\\,w={z}^{3}.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 2,\\,1\\le w\\le 8\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3{z}^{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3{z}^{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (3{z}^{2})-3\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =3{z}^{2} \\\\  & =3{w}^{2/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{3{w}^{2/3}}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |\\frac{1}{3{w}^{2/3}}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{8}{w}^{-2/3}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{8}{w}^{-2/3}dw \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[u]_{u=0}^{u=2}\\cdot [v]_{v=0}^{v=2}\\cdot [3{w}^{1/3}]_{w=1}^{w=8} \\\\  & =(2-0)\\cdot (2-0)\\cdot ({8}^{1/3}-{1}^{1/3}) \\\\  & =(2-0)\\cdot (2-0)\\cdot (2-1) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482616 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18ec478d-8c40-5efa-92a1-86ea6f027a90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(e-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481843} {:answer/id #uuid "24ed6cbd-b045-57ee-bdcf-da55c1ab6bff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481871} {:answer/id #uuid "6b962412-6d6a-5fe6-8c93-21ebb16264be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482021} {:answer/id #uuid "6c34f66d-0108-524b-a0bb-ea16f3872c5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482024} {:answer/id #uuid "b92bb4be-1ea9-57c7-8327-65aa672b6f3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482215}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18ec478d-8c40-5efa-92a1-86ea6f027a90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(e-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481843} :answer-field/id #uuid "80cdcf0b-fbd2-5ef6-980d-ca6cc3566902" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482447}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fd1760e0-c4d7-5ae6-945d-3f3e267b54eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-162170" :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le {y}^{2}-x\\le 2,\\,-1\\le y\\le 1,\\,1\\le z\\le e\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n1\\le {y}^{2}-x\\le 2,\\,-1\\le y\\le 1,\\,1\\le z\\le e,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu={y}^{2}-x,\\,v=y,\\,w=z.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,-1\\le v\\le 1,\\,1\\le w\\le e\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2y & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |-1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{e}1\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}du\\cdot {\\int }_{-1}^{1}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{e}dw \\\\  & =[u]_{u=1}^{u=2}\\cdot [v]_{v=-1}^{v=1}\\cdot [w]_{w=1}^{w=e} \\\\  & =(2-1)\\cdot (1-(-1))\\cdot (e-1) \\\\  & =2(e-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482617 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16ac1ac8-5d12-5b50-934c-de82720f574b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481839} {:answer/id #uuid "1a3281f8-9551-52d3-8d0c-c6b89b2d2548" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1,1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481844} {:answer/id #uuid "22e163e2-c51b-5f8c-acce-093bdea2aa79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0,1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481868} {:answer/id #uuid "4dde1e24-43fb-5f71-b240-852e369c1dd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1,0,1,\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481963} {:answer/id #uuid "c0cdcb5b-73ea-5836-bf70-b1a7b83f23d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1,\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482233}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a3281f8-9551-52d3-8d0c-c6b89b2d2548" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1,1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481844} :answer-field/id #uuid "68323756-6915-5a2a-b83f-b1d14e9cdd18" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482429}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fed1e82d-9029-565f-bbd7-a48e9974d84e" :question/math-academy-id "q-127090" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What are the modes of $X?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{96}{595}({x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}-2), & -1\\le x\\le \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The modes of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maxima of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $-1\\le x\\le \\frac{3}{2}$ is $f(x)=-\\frac{96}{595}({x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}-2).$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{96}{595}(4{x}^{3}-4x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{384}{595}x({x}^{2}-1),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n-\\frac{384}{595}x({x}^{2}-1)=0\\,⟹\\,x=0,\\pm 1.\n$$\n\nAll of these critical points are within the interval $[-1,\\frac{3}{2}],$ so we keep them all.\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoints $x=\\pm 1.$ So, these are critical points as well, but $-1$ is included above.\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points $x=0,\\pm 1,\\frac{3}{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =-\\frac{96}{595}((-1)^{4}-2(-1)^{2}-2)=\\frac{288}{595} \\\\ f(0) & =-\\frac{96}{595}((0)^{4}-2(0)^{2}-2)=\\frac{192}{595} \\\\ f(1) & =-\\frac{96}{595}((1)^{4}-2(1)^{2}-2)=\\frac{288}{595} \\\\ f(\\frac{3}{2}) & =-\\frac{96}{595}({(\\frac{3}{2})}^{4}-2{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-2)=\\frac{138}{595}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest values are\n\n$$\nf(-1)=f(1)=\\frac{288}{595}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the modes of $X$ are $-1$ and $1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482716 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76973ccd-ce2c-5c47-9012-95953c70b708" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482714}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76973ccd-ce2c-5c47-9012-95953c70b708" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482714} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ec1cc2e-eb99-5092-9662-f13d832f50bb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482710}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8f654224-65f2-57a8-af63-8ce90f4b8444" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{20}}9 \\sin{\\left(10 x \\right)}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=10 x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=10\\,\\Longrightarrow\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{10}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi}{20}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{20}}9 \\sin{\\left(10 x \\right)}\\,\\text{d}x &= {\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}9\\sin(u)\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\ &= \\frac{9}{10}{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\sin(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\ &= - \\frac{9 \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{10}\\big|_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\\\ &= 0-(- \\frac{9}{10}) \\\\ &= \\frac{9}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482722 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19d7216e-01c1-55dc-afe1-24eba218c8fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482709} {:answer/id #uuid "77ae08f7-2b6b-5e95-8284-a321136f9e0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482715} {:answer/id #uuid "b5e91fc1-2ced-55cf-849f-07ffaef0ddeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482719} {:answer/id #uuid "d29f8cd2-dee8-59dc-a36c-2b21b5dd77d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482721} {:answer/id #uuid "e5a92fef-96fc-50b1-a22d-d70a20e3848a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482723}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d29f8cd2-dee8-59dc-a36c-2b21b5dd77d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482721} :answer-field/id #uuid "4487d6e6-4e17-5c90-b1bb-1610fc9f5628" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482712}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d6b42e8b-fdca-50f0-be23-a0ae2f737155" :question/problem "Calculate ${\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{27}}\\sin{\\left(9 x + \\frac{\\pi}{3} \\right)}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=9 x + \\frac{\\pi}{3},$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=9\\,\\Longrightarrow\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{9}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi}{27}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\frac{\\pi}{3}$ | $\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{27}}\\sin{\\left(9 x + \\frac{\\pi}{3} \\right)}\\,\\text{d}x &= {\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}}1\\sin(u)\\cdot \\frac{1}{9}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{9}{\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}}\\sin(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\ &= - \\frac{\\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{9}\\big|_{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}} \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{18}-(- \\frac{1}{18}) \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482724 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55bc9615-3a10-584e-a34c-31b663bcb68a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482713}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55bc9615-3a10-584e-a34c-31b663bcb68a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482713} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3485420-0f9b-591f-a441-d93100d3c590" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482720}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f49ac21a-2d62-566e-a42d-88814c785a3f" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{48}}9 \\sin{\\left(24 x \\right)}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=24 x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=24\\,\\Longrightarrow\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{24}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi}{48}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{48}}9 \\sin{\\left(24 x \\right)}\\,\\text{d}x &= {\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}9\\sin(u)\\cdot \\frac{1}{24}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\ &= \\frac{3}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\sin(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\ &= - \\frac{3 \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{8}\\big|_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\\\ &= 0-(- \\frac{3}{8}) \\\\ &= \\frac{3}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482745 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7bb039d6-baeb-5e69-b420-94e2d9456f9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\ln (\\frac{49}{50})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482762} {:answer/id #uuid "979a18ba-450a-529e-8453-4d3240a96c05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln 49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482767} {:answer/id #uuid "a14be10f-099b-5fb4-8304-fb4ccaaf3af1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln (\\frac{7}{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482770} {:answer/id #uuid "c0e86b9d-0ced-5705-968a-d6339899ec18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln (\\frac{3}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482781} {:answer/id #uuid "c726e65a-fa91-52ae-81e3-33371a59bce6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln (\\frac{15}{7})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482783}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c726e65a-fa91-52ae-81e3-33371a59bce6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln (\\frac{15}{7})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482783} :answer-field/id #uuid "1669ebf4-a235-5e7b-a7e3-4ddcf9c0f902" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482731}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "468021ed-16bf-5dbe-89e2-8150c29c2812" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral\n$\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\ln 7}\\frac{\\cosh x}{3 + \\sinh x}dx$." :question/worked-solution "Let $u=3+\\sinh x$. Differentiating gives $\\mathrm{d}u=\\cosh x\\,\\mathrm{d}x$.\n\nWhen $x=0$, $u=3$. When $x=\\ln 7$,\n\n$$u=3+\\frac{e^{\\ln 7}-e^{-\\ln 7}}{2}\n=3+\\frac{7-\\frac{1}{7}}{2}=\\frac{45}{7}.$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_0^{\\ln 7}\\frac{\\cosh x}{3+\\sinh x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n&=\\int_3^{\\frac{45}{7}}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\n&=\\left[\\ln u\\right]_3^{\\frac{45}{7}} \\\\\n&=\\ln(\\frac{45}{7})-\\ln 3 \\\\\n&=\\ln\\left(\\frac{15}{7}\\right).\n\\end{aligned}$$\n\nHere $u>0$ throughout the interval, so the logarithm and substitution are defined."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482750 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16c423b4-ba29-504b-943b-b80e6351bf13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln (\\frac{3}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482732} {:answer/id #uuid "1ae8ab13-9351-5caf-a0eb-f91febdbcdbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln (\\frac{9}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482734} {:answer/id #uuid "7d42f666-6187-5cb4-b5d0-8327d320fcde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\ln (\\frac{25}{26})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482764} {:answer/id #uuid "894ce3d0-0c1e-5e6c-987c-24c108d13d7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln 25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482766} {:answer/id #uuid "cae9b7bb-eaf1-53e3-a1f5-14c462bfe22e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln (\\frac{5}{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482785}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ae8ab13-9351-5caf-a0eb-f91febdbcdbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln (\\frac{9}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482734} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c4b3775-e454-5cf7-a860-715e66896683" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482748}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5a0bd7c9-0123-5ee5-b41e-93cdcb9f8034" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral\n$\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\ln 5}\\frac{\\cosh x}{3 + \\sinh x}dx$." :question/worked-solution "Let $u=3+\\sinh x$. Differentiating gives $\\mathrm{d}u=\\cosh x\\,\\mathrm{d}x$.\n\nWhen $x=0$, $u=3$. When $x=\\ln 5$,\n\n$$u=3+\\frac{e^{\\ln 5}-e^{-\\ln 5}}{2}\n=3+\\frac{5-\\frac{1}{5}}{2}=\\frac{27}{5}.$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_0^{\\ln 5}\\frac{\\cosh x}{3+\\sinh x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n&=\\int_3^{\\frac{27}{5}}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\n&=\\left[\\ln u\\right]_3^{\\frac{27}{5}} \\\\\n&=\\ln(\\frac{27}{5})-\\ln 3 \\\\\n&=\\ln\\left(\\frac{9}{5}\\right).\n\\end{aligned}$$\n\nHere $u>0$ throughout the interval, so the logarithm and substitution are defined."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482753 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35375c10-89f4-5286-9a29-9054aa291235" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln 9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482738} {:answer/id #uuid "612c68f2-fc81-5183-8b91-8d5ad1c55cb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln (\\frac{3}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482752} {:answer/id #uuid "b1dcb43b-8e7a-53e3-a1a7-24fb2641144a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln (\\frac{3}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482776} {:answer/id #uuid "bf81bce8-d9f3-5e25-82ea-a08888e56ce7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln (\\frac{13}{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482779} {:answer/id #uuid "ff1ca937-eec1-50ff-a64f-cfa92f36eea3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\ln (\\frac{9}{10})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482795}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf81bce8-d9f3-5e25-82ea-a08888e56ce7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln (\\frac{13}{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482779} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae79f7c7-8d52-5e34-ac4c-26c1fa3a1da9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482775}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "639b907c-3a15-5f78-a8f4-1ad717b650eb" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral\n$\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\ln 3}\\frac{\\cosh x}{3 + \\sinh x}dx$." :question/worked-solution "Let $u=3+\\sinh x$. Differentiating gives $\\mathrm{d}u=\\cosh x\\,\\mathrm{d}x$.\n\nWhen $x=0$, $u=3$. When $x=\\ln 3$,\n\n$$u=3+\\frac{e^{\\ln 3}-e^{-\\ln 3}}{2}\n=3+\\frac{3-\\frac{1}{3}}{2}=\\frac{13}{3}.$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_0^{\\ln 3}\\frac{\\cosh x}{3+\\sinh x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n&=\\int_3^{\\frac{13}{3}}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\n&=\\left[\\ln u\\right]_3^{\\frac{13}{3}} \\\\\n&=\\ln(\\frac{13}{3})-\\ln 3 \\\\\n&=\\ln\\left(\\frac{13}{9}\\right).\n\\end{aligned}$$\n\nHere $u>0$ throughout the interval, so the logarithm and substitution are defined."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482780 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3db46bb1-ac6e-54c4-9ebe-35705f6a58cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\ln (\\frac{16}{17})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482740} {:answer/id #uuid "472e87e4-74c1-5005-8c9a-a499a58a14f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln (\\frac{3}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482746} {:answer/id #uuid "c3d0cde4-f50b-56fe-b792-ac8221f724ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln (\\frac{4}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482782} {:answer/id #uuid "dde17509-aaa7-56ca-ae80-5ab124f69c2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln 16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482787} {:answer/id #uuid "e946d67f-0c85-5adc-8926-5cb176fa01f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln (\\frac{13}{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482789}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e946d67f-0c85-5adc-8926-5cb176fa01f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln (\\frac{13}{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482789} :answer-field/id #uuid "3eacd647-34b8-5d12-b736-6395fc30e69a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482742}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c05537f7-1d5d-5292-9d77-e4dd0daf2542" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral\n$\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\ln 4}\\frac{\\cosh x}{3 + \\sinh x}dx$." :question/worked-solution "Let $u=3+\\sinh x$. Differentiating gives $\\mathrm{d}u=\\cosh x\\,\\mathrm{d}x$.\n\nWhen $x=0$, $u=3$. When $x=\\ln 4$,\n\n$$u=3+\\frac{e^{\\ln 4}-e^{-\\ln 4}}{2}\n=3+\\frac{4-\\frac{1}{4}}{2}=\\frac{39}{8}.$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_0^{\\ln 4}\\frac{\\cosh x}{3+\\sinh x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n&=\\int_3^{\\frac{39}{8}}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\n&=\\left[\\ln u\\right]_3^{\\frac{39}{8}} \\\\\n&=\\ln(\\frac{39}{8})-\\ln 3 \\\\\n&=\\ln\\left(\\frac{13}{8}\\right).\n\\end{aligned}$$\n\nHere $u>0$ throughout the interval, so the logarithm and substitution are defined."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482791 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cccaeda-6ecb-51bb-a2a1-8a0550dfcef2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\ln (\\frac{36}{37})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482729} {:answer/id #uuid "2277d424-532e-5ade-9081-473385c6ab7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln (\\frac{6}{7})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482735} {:answer/id #uuid "289329bc-db55-55d0-9ee5-8eb6f355da1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln (\\frac{3}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482737} {:answer/id #uuid "6ad8cd24-1e02-5134-88d3-3a32a9ee13a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln (\\frac{71}{36})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482754} {:answer/id #uuid "731dcae3-1742-5a64-930f-1919e898289a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln 36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482760}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ad8cd24-1e02-5134-88d3-3a32a9ee13a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln (\\frac{71}{36})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482754} :answer-field/id #uuid "77e1a9de-780d-510c-8e0f-915271c2447a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482761}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ec2ce764-8489-5f9a-a8a6-a50e646dc3d8" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral\n$\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\ln 6}\\frac{\\cosh x}{3 + \\sinh x}dx$." :question/worked-solution "Let $u=3+\\sinh x$. Differentiating gives $\\mathrm{d}u=\\cosh x\\,\\mathrm{d}x$.\n\nWhen $x=0$, $u=3$. When $x=\\ln 6$,\n\n$$u=3+\\frac{e^{\\ln 6}-e^{-\\ln 6}}{2}\n=3+\\frac{6-\\frac{1}{6}}{2}=\\frac{71}{12}.$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_0^{\\ln 6}\\frac{\\cosh x}{3+\\sinh x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n&=\\int_3^{\\frac{71}{12}}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\n&=\\left[\\ln u\\right]_3^{\\frac{71}{12}} \\\\\n&=\\ln(\\frac{71}{12})-\\ln 3 \\\\\n&=\\ln\\left(\\frac{71}{36}\\right).\n\\end{aligned}$$\n\nHere $u>0$ throughout the interval, so the logarithm and substitution are defined."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483346 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1554fed7-affd-52c8-9b9d-11f75d6528f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482850} {:answer/id #uuid "82a88aba-0a9e-5b36-8e60-a1e8c2073da2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483040} {:answer/id #uuid "b08ae541-af63-5e99-b15c-65086e6b98be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483112} {:answer/id #uuid "eda9481b-28f6-5a31-b610-2b56f853aa8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483212} {:answer/id #uuid "f6906166-88a4-5fdd-88ad-88c8265bc930" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483229}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82a88aba-0a9e-5b36-8e60-a1e8c2073da2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483040} :answer-field/id #uuid "ead372e1-7a0e-5867-a1cd-4c08293d91a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483332}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "04ab001d-eb12-502f-a292-54d2ac24e44a" :question/math-academy-id "q-106761" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\n{B}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{B}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,A=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -18 \\\\ 15 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIf $B=\\{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}\\},$ and $[A]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},$ then ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{B}_{1}+{x}_{2}{B}_{2}=A.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -18 \\\\ 15 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} & -2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} & 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -18 \\\\ 15 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nEquating the corresponding entries, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=9 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=-18 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=15.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 9 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -18 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 15\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 9 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -18 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 15\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{3}{2}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -9 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=9 \\\\ 3{x}_{2} &=-9 \\\\ -\\frac{{x}_{2}}{2} &=\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second and third equations, we get ${x}_{2}=-3.$ From the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} & =9 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+(-3) & =9 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $[A]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=6+(-3)=3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483352 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d1c4079-2e4b-58ce-bcdd-cef5dccc4c25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{\\theta }\\sqrt{2{\\theta }^{2}+2\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482832} {:answer/id #uuid "2dfd78b7-9d7a-58b9-8cb4-d7bec505415d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{\\theta {e}^{\\theta }+1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482890} {:answer/id #uuid "33feb924-d253-5720-9471-b530f5057780" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{\\theta }\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482903} {:answer/id #uuid "4170ee29-2690-59a0-ac02-7ef811bc4abc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}{e}^{2\\theta }+1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482922} {:answer/id #uuid "42a9d64f-13f1-500c-89f4-8b8e1616488f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{2\\theta }\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}+2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482925}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d1c4079-2e4b-58ce-bcdd-cef5dccc4c25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{\\theta }\\sqrt{2{\\theta }^{2}+2\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482832} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8b124e2-32da-5651-8541-39b8419c32ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483305}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "22b679e5-bd2f-5f85-b6e8-97dc0feb70a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-149799" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=\\theta {e}^{\\theta }\\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\,?$" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\theta {e}^{\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=(1+\\theta ){e}^{\\theta }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{(\\theta {e}^{\\theta })}^{2}+{((1+\\theta ){e}^{\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}{e}^{2\\theta }+(1+\\theta )^{2}{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{e}^{2\\theta }({\\theta }^{2}+1+2\\theta +{\\theta }^{2})}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{\\theta }\\sqrt{2{\\theta }^{2}+2\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483357 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a4c5101-e4ab-510b-a90b-1f80dc5e7721" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{49}{81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482863}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a4c5101-e4ab-510b-a90b-1f80dc5e7721" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{49}{81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482863} :answer-field/id #uuid "30583036-b5c2-5e98-859d-3145c20f317d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483252}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "319f568f-1d7f-56e6-aaee-eed8bdcd4507" :question/math-academy-id "q-166804" :question/problem "Expressed as a single fraction, the exact area of the plot of land is$\\,${{field-1}}\nhectares." :question/worked-solution "The exact area of land is ${(\\frac{7}{9})}^{2}$ hectares.\n\nWriting ${(\\frac{7}{9})}^{2}$ with multiplication signs, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{7}{9})}^{2} & =\\underbrace{\\frac{7}{9}\\times \\frac{7}{9}}_{2\\text{ times}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\times 7}{9\\times 9} \\\\  & =\\frac{49}{81}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{7}{9})}^{2}=\\frac{49}{81}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483358 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15ba5d73-cfbf-5d35-89f4-a709f6e9f4ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482852} {:answer/id #uuid "2c669c68-aefe-5e7c-b2c5-c63d2b25d4ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482885} {:answer/id #uuid "3c34cd61-57c9-53a6-a30c-553a2947ad18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482915} {:answer/id #uuid "700bdd4a-0eec-5a10-a0a6-451aa0f53abd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483006} {:answer/id #uuid "76a67f28-627f-53d2-bf6b-8d0da5300ff8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483019}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15ba5d73-cfbf-5d35-89f4-a709f6e9f4ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482852} :answer-field/id #uuid "3589368a-bcb8-5009-b31d-b69be611aa69" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483255}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "35226f58-033d-50fc-9ed5-94472de795ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-128060" :question/problem "Find the value of $(-\\frac{3}{8})÷\\frac{1}{6}.$" :question/worked-solution "Dividing a negative number by a positive number gives a negative number. Hence, the result will be negative.\n\nTherefore, since \n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{8}÷\\frac{1}{6}=\\frac{3\\times 6}{8\\times 1}=\\frac{18}{8}=\\frac{9}{4},\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{3}{8})÷\\frac{1}{6}=-\\frac{9}{4}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483367 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07e65c36-be44-5f01-9c76-e2d474ae0429" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{16+10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482823} {:answer/id #uuid "416c60e9-e558-531b-9580-88325c39f159" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{18-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482921} {:answer/id #uuid "d1393249-3bf9-5edc-8048-09a57dbf1249" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{8-18\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483160} {:answer/id #uuid "d1fcdafd-bf7d-5de0-8c88-50a987f3891f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{26-10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483163} {:answer/id #uuid "e65466c4-bccb-5f49-930c-7f3a5aefd1ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{10-26\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483201}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1fcdafd-bf7d-5de0-8c88-50a987f3891f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{26-10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483163} :answer-field/id #uuid "86b07591-e098-5b5d-80e3-d30542d3cd68" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483290}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "49e7f17d-b251-56fb-a73b-9aa663a5feb5" :question/math-academy-id "q-123410" :question/problem "![](images/ef/ef5fe44009ea42457243052cff584a216ee94636d8e3c16ff9446e85b8eca551.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=1-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{7\\pi }{6},$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=1-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{(1-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{1-10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +25{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +25{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{1-10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +25}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{26-10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483373 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "500ad374-633d-5acc-8fc6-2c954b7ceee0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482954} {:answer/id #uuid "7daf2602-5ddf-5b93-9c8f-85bf86b1183f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483033} {:answer/id #uuid "c4195c77-54de-55db-b8bc-7aa9584420e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483138} {:answer/id #uuid "dd638516-5b00-5d2e-b7d7-70996ad3e87f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483185} {:answer/id #uuid "eb789a46-0304-547b-8796-b1b162722c73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483207}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7daf2602-5ddf-5b93-9c8f-85bf86b1183f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483033} :answer-field/id #uuid "416c49b7-a3b7-5531-9507-24f1c4541b91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483261}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6017b841-30bb-5249-81e7-cac41367e88a" :question/math-academy-id "q-123548" :question/problem "![](images/b9/b9ea317575efcb32d8234309e80fecfd4385c99dbe69dd9c33d3fa7d80313bb6.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we evaluate our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{(2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{4+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2}){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =8(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483377 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a8a0905-3f5a-57f9-b71d-c2127323b463" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482943} {:answer/id #uuid "5b08ebea-7284-5412-99de-e04b22ab6d40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4+\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482971} {:answer/id #uuid "5db37158-5959-5928-a4a3-8612494e2e92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482973} {:answer/id #uuid "7a1781db-53be-5369-8346-96df148295ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483025} {:answer/id #uuid "d185a6d1-cc28-52e1-8c76-58d00373669d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3+\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483162}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d185a6d1-cc28-52e1-8c76-58d00373669d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3+\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483162} :answer-field/id #uuid "346d1a8e-e297-5ff1-8dff-7d5740531d20" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483254}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "75ca7d55-6e3f-58c8-8a50-805ce5d1a756" :question/math-academy-id "q-41838" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F={x}^{2}yz\\,i+{x}^{3}y\\,j+z\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})\\,k.$ Evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(-1,-1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}yz)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(z\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})) \\\\  & =2xyz+{x}^{3}+\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(-1,-1,2),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F(-1,-1,2) & =2(-1)(-1)(2)+(-1)^{3}+\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{-1}{-1}) \\\\  & =4-1+\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =3+\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483379 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04d839b3-7560-5512-ab94-65b25afb8af3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482817} {:answer/id #uuid "0bd0b7d8-497a-5308-9309-036b9effbae0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482828} {:answer/id #uuid "11be0602-805c-5751-80e1-20619f10d315" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{4}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482841} {:answer/id #uuid "1663c28c-4cc3-57a0-901a-0782cd1f5920" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482854} {:answer/id #uuid "e62cdbaf-ad20-51f2-b72a-b2f5ab5a24eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483200}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1663c28c-4cc3-57a0-901a-0782cd1f5920" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482854} :answer-field/id #uuid "63f7e3dc-e6fb-53cc-b486-dbf54e61e1b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483273}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "80893bd5-f8f9-5d69-b7d0-36e40ddfd184" :question/math-academy-id "q-76572" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=2$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}?$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=x{y}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot (2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(2)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{(2)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483391 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0edad87c-2a7e-5ce2-948c-23ebc4486f11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482835} {:answer/id #uuid "87f3dc21-f9fe-515d-b7a6-aa328708748c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483051} {:answer/id #uuid "8bd1a042-f0e1-5280-a93f-79b975c4ee9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483058} {:answer/id #uuid "a5057d38-1225-50a0-b6ad-25ed31fa235e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483092} {:answer/id #uuid "ed0e24a1-111c-5e17-8f98-c82fe143a3a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483210}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8bd1a042-f0e1-5280-a93f-79b975c4ee9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483058} :answer-field/id #uuid "58cd874b-d401-5dae-a003-642f47b6d5e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483268}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a8c3fd0e-01dd-58d7-b5d0-e6ecb2b5f479" :question/math-academy-id "q-65054" :question/problem "Evaluate ${f}_{zyx}(1,1,-1)$ for $f(x,y,z)=xy\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the first-order derivative ${f}_{z}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xy\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)) \\\\  & =xy\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)) \\\\  & =\\frac{xy}{x+y+z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find ${f}_{zy}(x,y,z)$ by differentiating ${f}_{z}(x,y,z)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{zy}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{z}(x,y,z)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{xy}{x+y+z}) \\\\  & =\\frac{x\\cdot (x+y+z)-xy\\cdot (1)}{(x+y+z)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+xy+xz-xy}{(x+y+z)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+xz}{(x+y+z)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find ${f}_{zyx}(x,y,z)$ by differentiating ${f}_{zy}(x,y,z)$ with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{zyx}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{zy}(x,y,z)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}+xz}{(x+y+z)^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(2x+z)\\cdot (x+y+z)^{2}-({x}^{2}+xz)\\cdot (2(x+y+z)\\cdot 1)}{(x+y+z)^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2x+z)(x+y+z)-2({x}^{2}+xz)}{(x+y+z)^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we evaluate ${f}_{zyx}(x,y,z)$ at $(1,1,-1):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{zyx}(1,1,-1) & =\\frac{(2(1)+(-1))(1+1+(-1))-2({1}^{2}+1(-1))}{(1+1+(-1))^{3}}=\\frac{(1)(1)-2(0)}{(1)^{3}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483393 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "424b2dd8-9b73-548c-b7cc-4ca500b6b156" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482924} {:answer/id #uuid "6f8353d3-9579-5c1e-8802-4ea1bdff86fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483004} {:answer/id #uuid "ab0fb722-71f0-5f53-8b20-486ab4bacd00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483102} {:answer/id #uuid "cd695a82-2dbd-573c-8f41-021965ec84e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483154} {:answer/id #uuid "ce613343-8043-5bc1-ae12-aad3ccc46878" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483158}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f8353d3-9579-5c1e-8802-4ea1bdff86fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483004} :answer-field/id #uuid "7774d184-d47b-5051-8e81-b32b977ef33a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483285}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ab43471e-dafe-5dcf-b8bc-06037ed994ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-128062" :question/problem "Determine the value of $(-\\frac{1}{5})÷(-\\frac{3}{10}).$" :question/worked-solution "Dividing a negative number by a negative number gives a positive number. Hence, the result will be positive.\n\nTherefore, since \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{5}÷\\frac{3}{10}=\\frac{1\\times 10}{5\\times 3}=\\frac{10}{15}=\\frac{2}{3},\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{1}{5})÷(-\\frac{3}{10})=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483419 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "614ec109-ddf5-5d69-8b6f-788cffd7d795" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482983} {:answer/id #uuid "654af1d0-1b91-562a-9226-854b3fe81f62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482990} {:answer/id #uuid "6bec234f-7e3b-515c-a2ad-33a2c8025357" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482999} {:answer/id #uuid "960e21ae-a0b1-56a4-a8e3-7aa555730f7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483072} {:answer/id #uuid "c60010fa-7943-5d78-8597-6d6fec062ebb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483143}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "614ec109-ddf5-5d69-8b6f-788cffd7d795" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482983} :answer-field/id #uuid "87114391-cb4d-5092-83cc-b2e18baed326" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483291}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "dcd1fd46-503a-5471-9362-496d88e411cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-149819" :question/problem "![](images/c7/c700a13da4a3b21940ec6268a89670eee850fc6206285bd3b444959a8b386276.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r={cos}^{3}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{3})$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2},$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r={cos}^{3}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{3}),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{3}){cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{3}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we evaluate our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{({cos}^{3}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{3}))}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{3}){cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{3}))}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{cos}^{6}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{3})+{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{3}){cos}^{4}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{3})}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{cos}^{4}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{3})[{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{3})+{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{3})]}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{cos}^{4}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{3})}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{3})\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{2}[1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\theta }{3})]\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[\\theta +\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\theta }{3})]}_{0}^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483421 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "236acfde-42f2-51a3-82ca-3600a09ce0a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482876} {:answer/id #uuid "38ff41e9-dd02-5d21-92a8-581369d18b20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482910} {:answer/id #uuid "8eac4e6e-fe81-5a84-9636-cabf10c5314f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483061} {:answer/id #uuid "acc1c101-f384-5d1e-ab31-e736b726cbf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483104} {:answer/id #uuid "b1c5c96c-0c63-5cb2-88af-e9d02ac33333" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483115}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8eac4e6e-fe81-5a84-9636-cabf10c5314f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483061} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf074d1f-8612-5220-b189-30860f156e17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483319}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e4160743-42ed-5b1d-80ee-6f99ed405134" :question/math-academy-id "q-149786" :question/problem "![](images/24/24f7ae31455abc10e60814b6b599966c92680422432c82f6cc7386d6d414f29c.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1)}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1+{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1+1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]
